    <body>
      <sec id="article-134015.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts represent a significant cause of preventable childhood visual impairment and blindness, necessitating early detection and timely intervention to support normal visual development. Presentation commonly includes leukocoria, strabismus, and nystagmus. Cataracts may be congenital, arising from genetic abnormalities or prenatal factors such as intrauterine infection, or acquired postnatally because of trauma, metabolic disease, or inflammatory conditions. Risk factors include family history, prematurity, systemic syndromes, and exposure to intrauterine toxins. Opacification of the crystalline lens interferes with visual pathway maturation and may lead to amblyopia and irreversible cortical visual deficits if treatment is delayed. Evaluation requires careful differentiation from other causes of leukocoria through comprehensive examination and appropriate imaging. Management strategies depend on etiology and severity and may include surgical extraction, optical rehabilitation, and long-term amblyopia therapy. Visual prognosis is closely linked to the timing of intervention, cataract density, and adherence to follow-up care.</p>
        <p>This educational activity enhances clinician competence in the evaluation and management of pediatric cataracts across the continuum of care. Participants develop a deeper understanding of disease etiology, pathophysiology, diagnostic approaches, and evidence-based treatment strategies, including postoperative visual rehabilitation. Instruction emphasizes risk assessment, interpretation of diagnostic findings, and anticipation of complications such as glaucoma and posterior capsule opacification. Collaboration within an interprofessional team, including pediatric specialists, optometrists, genetic counselors, and rehabilitation professionals, supports coordinated care, improves adherence to therapy, and optimizes long-term visual outcomes for affected children.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Develop coordinated care plans through effective communication and collaboration with the interprofessional healthcare team to support comprehensive management.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Compare congenital and acquired etiologies of pediatric cataracts to guide appropriate diagnostic and management strategies.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based guidelines to the diagnosis and management of pediatric cataracts to optimize visual development and long-term outcomes.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement effective collaboration and communication strategies among interprofessional team members to improve outcomes and treatment efficacy for pediatric individuals with cataracts.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=134015&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=134015">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts are a leading cause of treatable childhood blindness. Delayed recognition and treatment are associated with substantial social, economic, and emotional consequences at the individual, familial, and community levels. Effective management remains challenging because prevention of irreversible amblyopia depends on early identification, timely diagnosis, and prompt intervention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Population-based vision screening programs and caregiver recognition of early signs, such as leukocoria and strabismus, facilitate timely diagnosis and treatment. Favorable visual outcomes depend on comprehensive preoperative assessment, accurate intraocular lens (IOL) power calculation, meticulous surgical technique, and coordinated postoperative care, including visual rehabilitation. Optimal management requires an interprofessional approach involving pediatrics, anesthesiology, ophthalmology, and optometry.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>Globally, pediatric cataracts&#x000a0;constitute&#x000a0;a major cause of preventable childhood blindness, particularly in low- and middle-income regions, where delayed diagnosis frequently results in advanced clinical presentations, including nystagmus, poor fixation, and dense lens opacities. Early surgical intervention is associated with improved visual outcomes, enhanced functional development, and reduced long-term socioeconomic burden. Pediatric cataracts account for approximately 5% to 20% of childhood blindness and severe visual impairment worldwide, with an estimated incidence of 1.8 to 3.6 per 10,000 children per year and a prevalence ranging from 1 to 15 per 10,000 children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>Population-based data show similar trends in high-income settings. Holmes et al reported a prevalence of 3 to 4 visually significant cataracts per 10,000 live births in the US.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;A study by Rahi et al in the UK reported a prevalence of 3.18 per 10,000 live births, whereas Nile et al reported an incidence of approximately 5 per 10,000 births in China. Despite regional differences in detection and reporting, consistent findings across studies indicate no significant laterality or sex-based predilection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Hereditary congenital cataracts have a prevalence of 8.3% and 25%, with approximately 75% of cases following an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern. Pathogenic variants in crystallin proteins, which are essential for maintaining lens transparency and refractive function, have been associated with several cataract subtypes, including nuclear, lamellar, zonular, and posterior polar. Nonsyndromic inherited cataracts frequently involve genetic alterations in crystallin or connexin genes. <italic toggle="yes">PITX3</italic> mutations are specifically linked to posterior polar cataracts, while <italic toggle="yes">PAX6</italic> alterations are associated with anterior polar cataracts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>Syndromic cataracts are linked to specific genetic defects, including &#x003b1;-galactosidase A in Fabry disease, galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase (<italic toggle="yes">GALT</italic>) in galactosemia, <italic toggle="yes">OCRL</italic> in Lowe (oculocerebrorenal) syndrome, and <italic toggle="yes">NHS</italic> in Nance&#x02013;Horan syndrome, a cataract&#x02013;dental disorder (see <bold>Image</bold>. Congenital Cataracts and Abnormal Galactose Metabolism). Maternal and congenital infections, particularly <italic toggle="yes">Toxoplasma gondii</italic>, rubella, cytomegalovirus, herpes simplex virus, and <italic toggle="yes">Treponema pallidum</italic> (syphilis)&#x02014;collectively known as TORCH pathogens&#x02014;are also major contributors to pediatric cataracts. B Mahalakshmi et al reported a high prevalence of TORCH infections&#x000a0;in the Indian subcontinent, with 20% of cases testing seropositive. Ocular trauma is another significant cause, accounting for 12% to 46% of pediatric cataract cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>Concerns exist regarding the higher incidence of complications, such as glaucoma, uveitis, dense posterior capsule opacification, and increased secondary interventions following primary IOL implantation in children younger than 2.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r10">[10]</xref> Primary IOL implantation in this age group has nevertheless demonstrated safety, with excellent long-term outcomes compared to aphakia and secondary IOL implantation after age 2. The myopic shift is generally well controlled; visual acuity outcomes are favorable; and the incidence of complications, such as glaucoma, uveitis, membrane formation, synechiae, and the need for secondary interventions, is lower than previously reported. Special care is necessary for infants younger than 6 months because of the increased risk of adverse events in smaller eyes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>The process of emmetropisation in children is typically complete by age 12, with axial length increasing from approximately 16.5 mm at birth to 23 mm by age 13. This growth proceeds in 3 phases:&#x000a0;rapid (0.46 mm/month from birth to 6 months), infantile (0.15 mm/month from 6 to 18 months), and juvenile (0.15 mm/month from 18 months to 12 years). Corneal curvature also changes significantly, with mean keratometry readings decreasing from approximately 51.2 D at birth to 43.5 D in adulthood. Consequently, IOL power selection in pediatric patients must account for axial elongation and the associated myopic shift. This consideration necessitates the use of pediatric-specific IOL power calculation formulas adapted for ongoing ocular growth. Sharp-edged IOLs are now widely preferred due to an association with lower rates of visual axis opacification (VAO). Compared with round-edged lenses, sharp-edged designs result in fewer neodymium-doped yttrium aluminum garnet laser capsulotomies,&#x000a0;occurring in&#x000a0;1 of 371 eyes compared with&#x000a0;4 of 371 eyes.</p>
        <p>Prompt management of pediatric cataracts is essential for optimal visual development. Most children with congenital or developmental cataracts require surgical intervention. Initial assessment of visual significance may be performed using the red reflex during distant direct ophthalmoscopy (see <bold>Image</bold>. Red Reflex). For visually significant cataracts, bilateral cases should be treated between 6 and 8 weeks of age, while unilateral cases require earlier intervention, typically between 4 and 6 weeks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The etiologies of pediatric cataracts are diverse, ranging from conditions of unknown origin to those associated with genetic or systemic disorders. Cataracts may be unilateral or bilateral, with most unilateral cases and a proportion of bilateral cases classified as idiopathic.</p>
        <p>Causes of congenital cataracts include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Intrauterine infections:&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">T gondii</italic>, rubella, cytomegalovirus, herpes simplex virus, or syphilis (eg, TORCHES)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r13">[13]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Drug-induced: Corticosteroids, especially prednisolone; ivacaftor, a potentiator of the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator used to treat cystic fibrosis; and topotecan, a topoisomerase I inhibitor&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r14">[14]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Metabolic disorders: Galactosemia, galactokinase deficiency, hypocalcemia,&#x000a0;or hypoglycemia&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r15">[15]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trauma:&#x000a0;Accidental&#x000a0;or nonaccidental</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Radiation exposure: Laser photocoagulation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Association with other ocular conditions: Microphthalmia, microcornea, aniridia, persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous, Peters anomaly, corneal guttae, coloboma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inherited without systemic abnormalities: Autosomal dominant (most common), autosomal recessive (more common in consanguineous families), and X-linked&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r16">[16]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Association&#x000a0;with other systemic conditions:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Chromosomal abnormalities: Trisomies&#x000a0;13, 18,&#x000a0;and 21; Turner&#x000a0;and Cri-du-chat syndromes</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cerebro-oculo-facio-skeletal syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Mitochondrial disorders, particularly&#x000a0;Complex I deficiency</p></list-item><list-item><p>Renal disease, specifically Lowe syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Skeletal disorders: Smith-Lemli-Opitz, Conradi, and Weill-Marchesani syndromes</p></list-item><list-item><p>Digital anomalies, such as syndactyly or polydactyly: Bardet-Biedl and Rubinstein-Taybi syndromes</p></list-item><list-item><p>Central nervous system abnormalities:&#x000a0;Zellweger and&#x000a0;Meckel-Gruber syndromes</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cardiac disease: Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Dermatologic disorders:&#x000a0;Cockayne and&#x000a0;Rothmund-Thomson syndromes, atopic dermatitis,&#x000a0;incontinentia pigmenti, progeria, ichthyosis, and ectodermal dysplasia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Dental anomalies:&#x000a0;Nance-Horan and Lenz syndromes&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Idiopathic</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Bilateral congenital cataracts may arise&#x000a0;in the following settings:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Idiopathic cases (~60%)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hereditary&#x000a0;transmission (~30%): Autosomal dominant (~75%; most common), autosomal recessive, and X-linked</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intrauterine infections (TORCHES)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Multisystem genetic disorders</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inborn errors of metabolism</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Endocrinopathies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Trauma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uveitic cataract</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ocular abnormalities</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Genetic conditions: Down and Turner syndromes, myotonic dystrophy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Metabolic disorders: Diabetes mellitus (vacuolar cataracts), hypoglycemia, hypocalcemia, galactosemia (oil droplet cataracts), Fabry disease (spoke-like cataracts), Zellweger syndrome, hypoparathyroidism (multicolored flecks), and Lowe syndrome (thin disciform cataracts)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Drug&#x000a0;reactions: Steroids, miotics, chlorpromazine, and amiodarone&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r18">[18]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts exhibit a wide range of etiologies and clinical manifestations.&#x000a0;A thorough etiologic evaluation is critical since&#x000a0;the specific cause dictates clinical management and long-term treatment planning. Recognizing laterality and identifying potential systemic associations are vital to delivering comprehensive care and optimizing visual outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r19">[19]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Classification of Pediatric Cataracts by Etiology and Systemic Association</bold>
</p>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts arise from a wide spectrum of underlying conditions, with laterality, associated systemic findings, and clinical context providing key clues to the underlying cause. Below is an overview of common causes encountered in clinical practice, emphasizing patterns relevant to diagnosis and management. Genetic or hereditary pediatric cataracts result from autosomal-dominant mutations in <italic toggle="yes">CRYAA</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">CRYBB2</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">GJA8</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">MIP</italic>, as well as from autosomal-recessive&#x000a0;or X-linked forms.</p>
        <p>These cataracts are frequently bilateral, often isolated, and may be associated with microphthalmia or other ocular anomalies. Metabolic disorders, such as galactosemia caused by GALT deficiency, galactokinase deficiency, hypocalcemia or hypoparathyroidism, and diabetes mellitus, can produce characteristic lens opacities, including the &#x0201c;oil droplet&#x0201d; or snowflake cataract. These cases are often bilateral, accompanied by systemic manifestations, and require urgent metabolic management, particularly in galactosemia. Cataracts associated with intrauterine or perinatal infections, ie, TORCH, may coexist with microcephaly, sensorineural deafness, or chorioretinitis. Opacities&#x000a0;are&#x000a0;often bilateral in rubella.</p>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts may arise in the context of systemic syndromes, including Down syndrome, in which cerulean cataracts are observed. Marfan syndrome is often associated with ectopia lentis, with or without cataract. Lowe syndrome, Nance&#x02013;Horan syndrome, and myotonic dystrophy are also implicated. Cataracts in these conditions are frequently accompanied by multisystem manifestations and may represent the presenting feature. Ocular developmental abnormalities, such as persistent fetal vasculature, aniridia, microphthalmia, and posterior or anterior lenticonus, typically present unilaterally and may necessitate early surgical intervention due to visual axis distortion.</p>
        <p>Trauma, including blunt trauma resulting in rosette cataract, penetrating injuries, and birth trauma (eg, forceps), represents the most common cause of unilateral pediatric cataract. Iatrogenic cataracts may occur following ocular surgery, such as vitrectomy, or secondary to steroid therapy, often manifesting as posterior subcapsular opacities. Therapeutic radiation, commonly administered in oncology patients, produces dose-dependent posterior subcapsular cataracts. Drug-induced lens opacities result from prolonged exposure to systemic corticosteroids, chlorpromazine, or, rarely, amiodarone. Idiopathic cataracts, ie, those without identifiable cause after complete evaluation, account for approximately 25% to 30% of pediatric cases. Pattern differences should be noted during patient evaluation. Most bilateral cataracts arise from genetic, metabolic, syndromic, or infectious causes, while unilateral cataracts generally result from trauma or developmental anomalies. Urgent evaluation is indicated for suspected galactosemia, TORCH infections, and syndromic associations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r20">[20]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Various epidemiological studies have been conducted to evaluate the causes of childhood blindness. Untreated pediatric cataracts account for approximately&#x000a0;7.4% to 15.3% of cases, with a higher burden in low-resource settings due to delayed diagnosis and access barriers.&#x000a0;The incidence and prevalence of pediatric cataracts vary by region, with lower rates in high-income countries and higher rates in low-income settings. Incidence ranges from 1.8 to 3.6 per 10,000 children annually, while prevalence ranges from 0.63 to 13.6 per 10,000 in low-income countries such as Bangladesh, Pakistan, and India, compared to 0.42 to 2.05 per 10,000 in high-income countries such as the US and the UK.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;No significant differences in prevalence have been observed based on sex or laterality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts are a&#x000a0;chief cause of treatable childhood blindness globally, with higher incidence rates reported in developing countries. If not promptly treated, the condition can result in severe visual impairment, affecting the child's quality of life and placing socioeconomic strain on the family and broader community. Pediatric cataracts account for an estimated 5% to 20% of childhood blindness and severe visual impairment worldwide. The global incidence ranges from 1.8 to 3.6 per 10,000 children annually, and prevalence is estimated at 1 to 15 per 10,000. Country-specific studies show varied prevalence rates.&#x000a0;In the US, the prevalence is estimated at 3 to 4 visually significant cataracts per 10,000 live births.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>The prevalence of pediatric cataracts in the UK is approximately 3.18 per 10,000 live births. In China, the incidence is around 5 per 10,000 births. Although geographic variations in prevalence exist, significant differences in laterality (ie, whether&#x000a0;1 or both eyes are affected) or sex are not commonly reported. Hereditary cataracts account for approximately 30% of all pediatric cases, with about 75% following an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern and the remainder inherited in an autosomal recessive or X-linked fashion. Specific genetic conditions associated with pediatric cataracts include Down and Turner syndromes,&#x000a0;as well as myotonic dystrophy. Several metabolic disorders also contribute to cataract development, including diabetes mellitus, hypoglycemia, hypocalcemia, galactosemia, Fabry disease, Zellweger syndrome, hypoparathyroidism, and Lowe syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Certain medications, such as steroids, miotics, chlorpromazine, and amiodarone, can induce cataracts in children, usually posterior subcapsular.&#x000a0;Trauma is another major cause, accounting for 12% to 46% of pediatric cases and representing the leading cause of unilateral pediatric cataracts.&#x000a0;Maternal and congenital infections, particularly those from the TORCHES group, are also notable contributors. A study conducted in the Indian subcontinent reported a high incidence of TORCH infections, with 20% of cases testing seropositive.&#x000a0;Rubella remains a major cause of disease in regions with incomplete vaccination coverage.</p>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts represent a critical public health concern that requires timely diagnosis and intervention to prevent long-term visual impairment.&#x000a0;Low- and middle-income countries carry a disproportionately higher burden of blindness, where trauma, infection, and genetic causes dominate.&#x000a0;Early detection and timely surgery dramatically improve outcomes.&#x000a0;A thorough understanding of the epidemiology, including genetic, metabolic, drug-induced, traumatic, and infectious cases, is essential for effective management and treatment planning.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r26">[26]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts have diverse causes, including heritable genetic defects, fetal or childhood developmental insults, and associated systemic syndromes. Autosomal dominant inheritance is the most common pattern seen in hereditary cataracts.&#x000a0;A genetic screening study conducted in Australia identified up to 51 genes and loci associated with the condition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Crystallin mutations (<italic toggle="yes">CRYAA, CRYAB</italic>) cause nuclear or lamellar cataracts via protein aggregation, while connexin mutations (<italic toggle="yes">GJA3, GJA8</italic>) lead to zonular cataracts from impaired metabolic coupling.&#x000a0;Mutations in genes encoding transcription factors, such as&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">PAX6</italic> (anterior polar cataracts), <italic toggle="yes">FOXE3, MAF</italic> (<italic toggle="yes">C-MAF</italic>), <italic toggle="yes">PITX3</italic> (posterior polar cataracts),&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">MIP</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">CRYAA</italic>, are frequently observed. Mutations in genes encoding crystallin and connexin are also common.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts cloud the eye's natural lens and impair vision. The multifactorial pathophysiology involves genetic, metabolic, infectious, and environmental factors that disrupt the lens's normal transparency and function. Genetic mutations contribute not only to the presence of pediatric cataracts but also to their phenotypic variability and severity. The affected genes encode proteins essential for lens structure, development, and transparency. Crystallins, for instance, are the primary structural proteins of the lens, maintaining its optical clarity and refractive index. Alterations in crystallin genes lead to protein misfolding or aggregation, resulting in distinct cataract morphologies, including nuclear, lamellar, zonular, and posterior polar types. Connexins form gap junctions that facilitate intercellular communication and metabolic balance within the lens. The disruption of these structures impairs homeostasis and contributes to lens opacification.</p>
        <p>Transcription factors such as <italic toggle="yes">PITX3</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">PAX6</italic> orchestrate lens morphogenesis and differentiation. Mutations in <italic toggle="yes">PITX3</italic> have been linked to posterior polar cataracts, while <italic toggle="yes">PAX6</italic> alterations are more commonly associated with anterior polar variants. These gene-specific effects highlight the complex molecular basis of pediatric cataracts and underscore the need for&#x000a0;genotype-phenotype correlation&#x000a0;in guiding diagnosis and management.</p>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts may also arise from underlying metabolic disorders. Diabetes mellitus causes elevated blood glucose levels, leading to osmotic imbalances in the lens that result in swelling and vacuole formation, ultimately contributing to cataractogenesis&#x000a0;(snowflake or diffuse cataracts). In galactosemia, accumulation of galactitol due to impaired galactose metabolism induces osmotic stress and leads to the formation of&#x000a0;oil droplet cataracts. Hypocalcemia, characterized by low calcium levels, disrupts lens cell function and protein stability, promoting cataract formation.&#x000a0;Fabry disease, a lysosomal storage disorder, is characterized by glycosphingolipid accumulation and is associated with spoke-like lens opacities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>Maternal and congenital infections, particularly TORCH infections, can lead to congenital cataracts in newborns. These infections induce intrauterine inflammation and damage the developing lens, resulting in opacification.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;Drug-induced cataracts may occur following prolonged exposure to specific medications. Corticosteroids, for instance, alter lens metabolism and protein structure, contributing to posterior subcapsular cataracts. Other drugs, such as miotics, chlorpromazine, and amiodarone, can induce lens opacities through oxidative stress or disruption of lens cell function. Trauma to the eye, whether accidental or intentional, can also result in cataract formation, ie, rosette or total cataracts from capsule breach and protein denaturation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>Environmental and nutritional factors play significant roles in the development of pediatric cataracts. Prolonged exposure to&#x000a0;UV radiation and other environmental stressors can heighten oxidative stress within the lens. This increased oxidative burden damages lens proteins and lipids, ultimately leading to cataract formation. Additionally, deficiencies in essential nutrients, particularly antioxidants such as vitamins C and E, can compromise lens health. Such deficiencies impair the lens&#x02019;s ability to counteract oxidative damage and contribute to cataract progression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>The mechanisms underlying lens opacification are multifaceted and involve several biological processes. Protein aggregation, often resulting from genetic mutations or metabolic imbalances, leads to the unfolding and clumping of crystallin proteins, thereby scattering light and impairing lens transparency. Osmotic imbalances, as seen in conditions such as diabetes and galactosemia, cause the accumulation of osmolytes within the lens, leading to cellular swelling, membrane rupture, and protein leakage. Disruption of calcium homeostasis further contributes to lens opacity by altering cell signaling and destabilizing lens proteins.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>Inflammatory responses induced by infections or trauma can damage lens cells and proteins, further promoting cataract formation. Given the complex pathophysiology of pediatric cataracts, which encompasses genetic mutations, metabolic disturbances, infections, drug toxicity, trauma, and environmental influences, a comprehensive understanding of these mechanisms is essential for developing effective preventive and therapeutic strategies to preserve vision in affected children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r35">[35]</xref><bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Genotype-phenotype correlation is increasingly crucial for diagnosis, prognosis, and counseling.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>The histopathology of pediatric cataracts involves the microscopic examination of lens tissue to characterize the structural changes that lead to opacification. Different cataract types exhibit distinct histopathological features, offering insight into their underlying mechanisms and informing potential treatment approaches. Pediatric cataracts commonly display disorganized and fragmented lens fibers. In healthy lenses, fibers are tightly packed and transparent.&#x000a0;However, in cataractous lenses, the&#x000a0;fibers may appear swollen, misaligned, or vacuolated. Aggregates of denatured or misfolded proteins, particularly crystallins, are often observed within lens fibers. These aggregates scatter light and contribute to lens opacity. Fluid-filled vacuoles are frequently seen in pediatric cataracts, especially those associated with metabolic disorders. These vacuoles arise from osmotic imbalance and lens cell damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>Genetic cataracts&#x000a0;manifest in various forms, each with characteristic histological features. Nuclear cataracts exhibit central lens opacity with dense protein aggregation and increased compaction of lens fibers in the nucleus. Lamellar cataracts present as disk-shaped opacities involving specific layers of the lens. Histologically, these layers contain swollen and disrupted fibers with embedded protein aggregates. Posterior polar cataracts are localized to the posterior aspect of the lens and involve abnormalities of the posterior capsule and adjacent lens fibers. Histologic findings include capsular fibrosis and aberrant cellular proliferation, both of which contribute to the observed opacification.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r37">[37]</xref></p>
        <p>Metabolic cataracts encompass a spectrum of conditions associated with metabolic disturbances, each exhibiting distinctive pathological features. Cataracts related to galactosemia are characterized by oil droplet formations within the lens, accompanied by vacuolization, osmotic swelling of lens fibers, and deposits of galactitol. Diabetic cataracts exhibit hallmark features, including vacuolar changes, fiber swelling, and sorbitol accumulation. The osmotic effect induced by intracellular sorbitol promotes water influx into the lens, leading to opacification. Cataracts associated with Fabry disease often present with spoke-like opacities, lipid accumulation, and abnormal glycosphingolipid deposits within lens fibers. These distinct histological findings underscore the complex relationship between metabolic abnormalities and lens pathology in metabolic cataracts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>Congenital infections, particularly those caused by TORCH pathogens, leave characteristic histopathological imprints on the lens. Microscopic examination typically reveals inflammation, fibrosis, and cellular infiltration within lens tissue. Thickening of the lens capsule may also be observed, along with viral or parasitic inclusion bodies, further highlighting the detrimental effects of congenital infections on lens development and integrity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>Traumatic cataracts result from physical injury to the eye and are characterized by various pathological changes within the lens. Capsule rupture, subsequent fibrosis, and abnormal cell proliferation are common posttraumatic findings. Disruption of the lens capsule's integrity often results in disorganized lens fibers and the development of opacities.&#x000a0;In some cases, calcium deposition within the lens further contributes to opacity and increases lens rigidity.&#x000a0;These pathological alterations underscore the diverse structural and functional consequences of trauma in the development of traumatic cataracts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r40">[40]</xref></p>
        <p>Certain medications can also induce cataract formation. Steroid-induced cataracts typically manifest as posterior subcapsular opacities, with histopathological findings of swollen and vacuolated lens epithelial cells. These changes result primarily from altered cellular metabolism and protein aggregation. Other drugs associated with cataract formation include miotics, chlorpromazine, and amiodarone, which produce histopathological features similar to those observed in cataract formation, including lens fiber swelling, vacuole formation, and protein aggregation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>Key histopathological techniques include light microscopy, electron microscopy, and immunohistochemistry. Light microscopy of stained lens tissue sections reveals structural changes in lens fibers, protein aggregates, and cellular abnormalities. Electron microscopy offers detailed views of subcellular structures, enabling the observation of crystallin protein aggregates, membrane disruptions, and intracellular vacuoles. Immunohistochemistry detects specific proteins and cellular markers, aiding the identification of genetic mutations and metabolic disturbances that contribute to cataract formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r42">[42]</xref></p>
        <p>The histopathology of pediatric cataracts reveals a range of structural and cellular abnormalities that contribute to lens opacification.&#x000a0;Understanding these distinct histological patterns aids etiological classification, prognosis, and targeted management.&#x000a0;Understanding these changes at the microscopic level is essential for diagnosing underlying causes and developing targeted treatments to restore vision in children with cataracts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r43">[43]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s7" sec-type="Toxicokinetics">
        <title>Toxicokinetics</title>
        <p>Pediatric cataract is not primarily a toxicological disease.&#x000a0;However, several etiologies&#x02014;particularly metabolic, drug-induced, infectious, and environmental exposures&#x02014;exhibit mechanisms analogous to toxicokinetics, including the absorption, distribution, metabolism, and accumulation of harmful substances or metabolic by-products that ultimately result in lens opacification. In this context, the term &#x0201c;toxicokinetics&#x0201d; in pediatric cataract describes how specific toxins, metabolites, or drugs reach the lens, interact with lens proteins, and induce structural changes that lead to cataract formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>General Principles of Toxicokinetics in Pediatric Cataract Formation</bold>
</p>
        <p>The pediatric lens is more metabolically active, osmotically vulnerable, and biochemically sensitive than the adult lens, allowing toxic insults to produce cataracts more rapidly and with greater severity in children. Key pathogenic mechanisms include intralenticular accumulation of metabolites or toxins; oxidative stress&#x02013;mediated damage to crystallin proteins; disruption of osmotic balance, with consequent lens hydration changes; impairment of enzymatic pathways essential for lens transparency; and direct deposition of drugs or toxins, leading to protein denaturation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r45">[45]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Toxicokinetics in Major Etiological Categories</bold>
</p>
        <p>Several major causes of pediatric cataract share a common pathophysiologic framework in which harmful substances or energy sources enter the ocular environment, persist within lens tissues, and disrupt normal lens architecture.&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Metabolic disorders</bold> represent the etiological category most closely aligned with true toxicokinetic mechanisms in pediatric cataract formation.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>In galactosemia caused by GALT deficiency, galactitol acts as the primary toxic agent. Dietary galactose is absorbed but cannot be metabolized via normal metabolic pathways, leading to its preferential conversion to galactitol via aldose reductase. Galactitol accumulates within lens fibers, producing osmotic swelling, fiber rupture, and the characteristic &#x0201c;oil droplet&#x0201d; cataract. Cataract formation may occur within days to weeks of birth and remains reversible in early stages with prompt dietary galactose restriction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r46">[46]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Galactokinase deficiency</bold> results in a similar toxicokinetic profile, with impairment of the initial metabolic step leading to more direct accumulation of galactitol and early bilateral cataract formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r47">[47]</xref> Diabetes mellitus, although an uncommon cause in children, induces&#x000a0;pathology through sorbitol accumulation generated by aldose reductase, resulting in osmotic stress, lens swelling, and rapidly progressive snowflake cataracts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r48">[48]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Drug-induced</bold> pediatric cataracts also demonstrate toxicokinetic behavior.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Systemic or topical corticosteroids alter lens epithelial cell gene expression and promote oxidative damage with protein aggregation, resulting in posterior subcapsular cataract formation.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Steroids readily cross the blood&#x02013;aqueous barrier, and chronic exposure increases intralenticular deposition.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Chlorpromazine binds directly to lens proteins, leading to pigment deposition and brown discoloration.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Amiodarone, although rarely used in pediatric populations, induces granular lens deposits through drug accumulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r49">[49]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Radiation-induced</bold> cataracts result from exposure to ionizing radiation, which generates free radicals that damage lens epithelial cell DNA and inhibit mitosis, leading to defective fiber differentiation and posterior subcapsular cataract formation. Children undergoing radiation therapy for conditions such as brain tumors or leukemia demonstrate heightened susceptibility.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r50">[50]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Infectious agents </bold>within the TORCH complex cause cataracts by&#x000a0;toxin-like intracellular mechanisms.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Rubella virus replicates within the lens, causing metabolic arrest and persistent viral antigen interference with crystallin development.</p></list-item><list-item><p>CMV, HSV, and toxoplasmosis release cytokines and toxic metabolites that disrupt normal lens fiber differentiation. These processes mimic intracellular toxic injury and result in congenital cataract formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r51">[51]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Environmental and chemical exposures</bold> provide less common but illustrative examples of true toxicokinetics.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Naphthalene exposure leads to the metabolism of naphthalene into naphthoquinones, thereby generating oxidative stress and cataractogenesis.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Heavy metals such as mercury and lead bind to sulfhydryl groups in lens proteins, thereby denaturing and opacifying the lens. Although rare in pediatric populations, these exposures demonstrate classical toxicokinetic pathways affecting the lens.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Absorption, Distribution, and Accumulation Considerations in Children</bold>
</p>
        <p>In children, the lens exhibits higher permeability, increasing susceptibility to toxins and harmful metabolites. A less mature blood&#x02013;aqueous barrier permits drugs and metabolic by-products to enter the lens more readily. Rapid cellular turnover within the pediatric lens facilitates faster accumulation of structural damage. In metabolic disorders, systemic accumulation of toxic metabolites is accompanied by intralenticular deposition, which further accelerates cataract formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r52">[52]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Elimination and Reversibility</bold>
</p>
        <p>Elimination and reversibility of pediatric cataracts depend on the underlying etiology and duration of exposure. Cataracts associated with early galactosemia may reverse following cessation of galactitol exposure through dietary galactose restriction. Diabetes-related cataracts may demonstrate partial improvement with adequate glycemic control. In contrast, radiation-&#x000a0;or steroid-induced, congenital TORCH-related, and longstanding metabolic cataracts are generally irreversible due to permanent structural damage to lens fibers and epithelial cells.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r53">[53]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r54">[54]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s8" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Pediatric cataract evaluation requires a meticulous approach that integrates a comprehensive history, a thorough ocular examination, and a systemic assessment. This multifaceted process is essential to identify potential underlying causes and associated ocular or systemic conditions. History-taking should elicit key details such as age of onset, symptom duration, antenatal and perinatal events (including maternal infections, exposures, and birth complications), developmental milestones, and behavioral signs of visual dysfunction (eg, inability to catch objects, frequent falls, photophobia).&#x000a0;The&#x000a0;evaluation should also include any history suggestive of systemic abnormalities, trauma, prior treatment or surgery, and family history of congenital or developmental cataracts or consanguinity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r55">[55]</xref>&#x000a0;A history of ocular trauma or systemic steroid use is also important.</p>
        <p>A detailed physical examination is equally important. The pediatric clinician should assess for systemic or genetic associations through careful inspection. For instance, head circumference measurement is particularly relevant in cases of congenital cataracts, as certain syndromes, such as&#x000a0;trisomy 21 and Hallermann-Streiff-Francois, Lowe (oculocerebrorenal), Cri-du-chat (5p deletion), Nance-Horan, and Edward syndromes, may present with both cataracts and abnormal head size.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r56">[56]</xref></p>
        <p>Reviewing family photos can be helpful when the exact onset of cataracts is unclear. Early cataracts that develop during critical periods of ocular and visual system maturation may be associated with a poorer prognosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r57">[57]</xref> Examination of the eyes of family members, particularly parents and siblings,&#x000a0;may reveal undiagnosed lenticular changes suggestive of inherited causes. Autosomal dominant inheritance is the most common pattern, although X-linked recessive transmission can also occur, as seen in conditions such as Lowe and Nance-Horan syndromes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r58">[58]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r59">[59]</xref></p>
        <p>A comprehensive ocular examination is essential for the assessment of pediatric cataract. Visual acuity testing should be tailored to the child's developmental level. In preverbal children, assessment tools may include the Bruckner test, cover test, resistance to occlusion of the better eye, monocular fixation, fixation preference, vertical prism test, forced-choice preferential looking, Teller acuity cards, optokinetic nystagmus, and pattern visual evoked potential.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r61">[61]</xref>&#x000a0;The Bruckner test, performed using a direct ophthalmoscope, enables simultaneous visualization of the red reflex in both eyes. This technique can detect anisometropia or unequal refractive power. In strabismus, the deviated eye typically shows a lighter red reflex than the fixating eye. Similarly, in anisometropia, one eye may display a brighter reflex than the other.</p>
        <p>Verbal children undergo a multifaceted assessment of visual acuity. Detection acuity testing, using tools such as the Catford drum test and the Standardized Tests for Young Children and Retardates (STYCAR) graded ball test, evaluates the ability to perceive light or motion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r62">[62]</xref>&#x000a0;Recognition acuity testing assesses the ability to identify specific visual stimuli. This&#x000a0;modality includes direction recognition using the Landolt C or Snellen E tests, letter identification with Snellen charts or high-contrast optotype visual acuity tests, and picture recognition with tools such as Allen picture cards or Domino charts.</p>
        <p>Behavioral pattern assessment, using methods such as the Cardiff Acuity Test and the optokinetic drum test, indirectly assesses visual function by observing the child&#x02019;s responses to visual stimuli.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r63">[63]</xref>&#x000a0;Fixation preference must be examined, along with the central-steady-maintained method in infants, and Teller or Cardiff acuity cards in preverbal children.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r64">[64]</xref> Fundus examination should be performed, with B-scan ultrasonography used if media opacity prevents direct visualization.</p>
        <p>The slit-lamp examination evaluates the anterior segment.&#x000a0;Cataract size, morphology, and location&#x02014;ie, nuclear, lamellar, polar, cortical, cerulean, or oil-droplet&#x02014;should be documented. Associated abnormalities, including microphthalmia, persistent fetal vasculature, iris coloboma, aniridia, lenticonus, microcornea, anterior segment dysgenesis, microspherophakia, ectopia lentis or lens subluxation, and preexisting posterior capsular defects, must be identified (see <bold>Image</bold>. Slit-Lamp Examination of Pediatric Zonular Cataract).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r65">[65]</xref>&#x000a0;The morphological classification of pediatric cataracts includes the following types:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Anterior: Anterior lenticonus (associated with Alport syndrome), anterior polar, anterior pyramidal, and anterior subcapsular</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>With involvement of&#x000a0;the entire lens: Total and membranous</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Posterior: Mittendorf dot, posterior lenticonus, and posterior subcapsular (see <bold>Image</bold>. Pediatric Cataract, Posterior Lenticonus)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Central: Lamellar (zonular), pulverulent, oil drop, coronary, blue dot</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sutural</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Wedge-shaped <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r66">[66]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Ocular motility evaluation assesses for strabismus and nystagmus, which should be specifically examined in children. These findings may represent early signs of pediatric cataracts and may indicate potential visual impairment. Strabismus commonly occurs with unilateral cataract and typically develops after significant visual loss has already occurred. Congenital sensory nystagmus is frequently associated with pediatric cataracts. The presence of either strabismus or nystagmus suggests that the cataracts are visually significant.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r68">[68]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r69">[69]</xref></p>
        <p>IOP measurement, performed with a tonometer, helps rule out glaucoma associated with congenital rubella syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r70">[70]</xref> Pupillary reaction testing provides a rough assessment of optic nerve head function. Direct and indirect ophthalmoscopy are used to evaluate for associated vitreous or posterior segment abnormalities, such as vitreous hemorrhage, persistent fetal vasculature, fundal coloboma, and optic or macular hypoplasia. Visually significant cataracts are typically centrally or posteriorly located and measure more than 3 mm in diameter. These cataracts often result in visual acuity of 20/60 or worse, reduced contrast sensitivity, increased glare, impaired stereoacuity, and associated strabismus or nystagmus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r71">[71]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r72">[72]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Examination of The Red Reflex</bold>
</p>
        <p>Medical professionals caring for children, especially newborns and neonates, must be proficient in performing the red reflex test. The American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) recommends that red reflex testing be performed in all neonates and at all routine well-child visits and healthcare appointments.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r73">[73]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r74">[74]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r75">[75]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r76">[76]</xref>&#x000a0;The red reflex is commonly observed in flash photographs and is essentially a reflection of the pupil. The retina reflects light entering the eye through the pupil and exits back through the optical media, producing a reddish glow. A normal red reflex confirms the transparency of the eye&#x02019;s optical structures, including the tear film, cornea, aqueous humor, lens, vitreous gel, and retina.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r77">[77]</xref> The test focuses on the reflex&#x02019;s presence or absence, as well as its color, brightness, and symmetry between the eyes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r78">[78]</xref></p>
        <p>Requirements for performing a red reflex test include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Dimming or turning off the room lights</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Positioning the child on the parent's lap so that the child&#x02019;s eyes are level with the examiner&#x02019;s</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Setting the direct ophthalmoscope diopter power to the examiner's refractive correction or to 0</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Holding the ophthalmoscope close to the examiner&#x02019;s eye and 12 to 18 inches away from the child&#x02019;s eyes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Encouraging the child to look at the light,&#x000a0;with help from&#x000a0;an assistant or toys if needed</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Directing the light at each eye individually to detect and compare the light reflexes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Assessing reflex symmetry by shining the light on both eyes simultaneously (Bruckner test)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r79">[79]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The red reflex examination&#x02019;s simplicity, speed, and noninvasiveness make it an essential tool for the early detection of pediatric cataracts.&#x000a0;Red reflex screening is recommended by the&#x000a0;American Association for Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus (AAPOS), American Academy of Ophthalmology (AAO), and US Preventive Services Task Force (USPSTF), with any abnormality prompting urgent referral to pediatric ophthalmology.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s9" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The evaluation of pediatric cataract requires a systematic, time-sensitive, and interprofessional approach. Early diagnosis is critical, as visual deprivation in the first months of life may lead to irreversible amblyopia. The evaluation aims to determine cataract characteristics, including type, density, location, and laterality; assess the impact on the visual axis; identify associated ocular abnormalities; and detect underlying systemic, genetic, or metabolic causes. In addition, the assessment includes determining the timing of intervention and surgical candidacy. Both the AAPOS and the Royal College of Ophthalmologists (UK) emphasize early examination and prompt intervention for visually significant cataracts. The initial assessment relies on a thorough history and a comprehensive eye examination to identify systemic associations and ocular comorbidities. These findings inform subsequent investigations and management decisions.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Imaging</bold>
</p>
        <p>B-scan ultrasonography is required when the fundus is not visible.&#x000a0;This modality&#x000a0;can exclude&#x000a0;posterior segment pathologies that may mimic congenital cataracts, such as retinoblastoma,&#x000a0;persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous,&#x000a0;Coats disease, retinopathy of prematurity with retrolental fibroplasia, organized vitreous hemorrhage, congenital falciform folds, ocular toxocariasis, and retinal hamartomas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r80">[80]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r81">[81]</xref>&#x000a0;The AAO Pediatric Cataract Guidelines recommend performing B-scan ultrasonography before cataract surgery if the posterior pole cannot be visualized.</p>
        <p>Ultrasound biometry is indicated for surgical planning, including measurement of axial length and keratometry, whether manual or automated.&#x000a0;This modality also&#x000a0;provides other essential optical parameters, such as anterior chamber depth and lens thickness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r82">[82]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r83">[83]</xref>&#x000a0;These measurements are required for IOL power calculation; pediatric formulas such as the Sanders-Retzlaff-Kraff/Theoretical or Holladay 1 are recommended, with particular caution in children younger than 2 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r84">[84]</xref></p>
        <p>Optical coherence tomography (OCT) is useful when media clarity permits. OCT detects macular hypoplasia, as in albinism or aniridia, aids in prognostication of visual outcomes, and evaluates epiretinal membranes or foveal development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r85">[85]</xref>&#x000a0;Keratometry, typically performed with handheld keratometers, requires the child&#x02019;s cooperation to obtain accurate readings. A standard K value of 43.00 D may be used if the child is uncooperative.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r86">[86]</xref> Magnetic resonance imaging or computed tomography is recommended when&#x000a0;persistent fetal vasculature&#x000a0;or retinoblastoma is suspected, as these modalities provide detailed visualization of the brain and orbits.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Laboratory Workup</bold>
</p>
        <p>Laboratory tests are indicated when a cataract appears metabolic, infectious, or syndromic, especially in the presence of bilateral&#x000a0;involvement, unclear origin, failure to thrive, hepatosplenomegaly, or jaundice. Evaluation should follow a structured approach, guided by clinical suspicion and the patient&#x02019;s presentation. Routine blood work should include a complete hemogram, blood glucose, and urine routine microscopy. Additional tests are obtained on a case-by-case basis, guided by the clinical history and examination findings. These&#x000a0;assessments may include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Serum calcium for hyperparathyroidism or hypoparathyroidism&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r87">[87]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Venereal Disease Research Laboratory testing for syphilis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r88">[88]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>TORCH infection antibody titers&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r89">[89]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Red cell galactokinase or uridyl transferase assays for galactosemia&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r90">[90]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Urine protein analysis for Alport syndrome&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r91">[91]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Urine amino acid screening for Lowe syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Urine sodium nitroprusside or plasma homocysteine for homocystinuria&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r92">[92]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Urine or serum copper levels for Wilson disease&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r93">[93]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Karyotyping to detect genetic abnormalities</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Herpes simplex or varicella virus zoster&#x000a0;testing</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Red blood cell GALT enzyme assay</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Urine-reducing substances</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Genetic&#x000a0;Analysis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Genetic testing is indicated for bilateral cataracts without metabolic or infectious causes, a positive family history, syndromic features, or associated ocular anomalies.&#x000a0;Evaluation&#x000a0;options include chromosomal microarray, targeted gene panels encompassing crystallin genes and connexins, such as <italic toggle="yes">GJA8</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">MIP</italic>, and whole-exome sequencing in complex cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r94">[94]</xref>&#x000a0;AAPOS and the Royal College recommend genetic evaluation for all cases of bilateral congenital cataracts, particularly when no definitive metabolic or infectious etiology is identified.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Interprofessional Assessment&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Additional evaluation considerations include consultations with various specialists. A pediatrician assesses systemic health, coordinates care, and evaluates for systemic signs, including growth retardation; cardiac abnormalities (eg, rubella); hearing loss (TORCH); musculoskeletal abnormalities (eg, Marfan syndrome); and renal disorders (eg, Lowe syndrome).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r95">[95]</xref> A geneticist should be consulted when hereditary conditions are suspected, providing genetic analysis and counseling. An endocrinologist may be involved when metabolic disorders that affect ocular health are suspected to ensure targeted management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r96">[96]</xref>&#x000a0;Neurology and developmental assessment are indicated for syndromes associated with neurological deficits, such as Down syndrome or congenital infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r97">[97]</xref></p>
        <p>Evaluation of surgical candidacy involves determining the visual significance of the cataract. Indicators for early surgery, according to AAPOS guidelines, include a central opacity measuring at least 3 mm, posterior cataracts involving the visual axis, a dense unilateral cataract, the presence of associated strabismus or nystagmus, and loss of fixation behavior.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r98">[98]</xref>&#x000a0;Preoperative assessment for pediatric cataracts requires a comprehensive anesthetic evaluation, particularly for infants and children with comorbidities, to ensure fitness for anesthesia. The preoperative workup also includes measurement of IOP, axial length, and keratometry values, assessment for potential IOL implantation, and evaluation for&#x000a0;persistent fetal vasculature&#x000a0;or zonular weakness, which may influence the surgical plan.</p>
        <p>Following diagnosis, follow-up testing is essential and includes amblyopia assessment in unilateral or asymmetric cataracts to determine the need for patching or penalization postsurgery,&#x000a0;IOP monitoring to detect steroid-induced or angle anomaly&#x02013;related glaucoma, and postoperative imaging with ultrasound or OCT for posterior segment evaluation, with repeat biometry performed in growing children if future surgery is anticipated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r99">[99]</xref>&#x000a0;Managing pediatric cataracts demands an interprofessional approach that integrates clinical, laboratory, and imaging assessments. Adherence to national and international guidelines helps standardize diagnosis and management, improving outcomes. Early detection and thorough evaluation are critical to preventing long-term visual impairment and enhancing the quality of life for affected children.</p>
        <p>
<bold>National and International Recommendations for Pediatric Cataract Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>National and international guidelines emphasize early detection and timely management of pediatric cataracts. The AAO and the AAPOS recommend universal ABR screening in the newborn period, with urgent evaluation of any abnormal reflex. Surgery is advised before 6 to 8 weeks for bilateral visually significant cataracts and before 4 to 6 weeks for unilateral cataracts, with lifelong follow-up due to the risk of glaucoma and refractive changes. The Royal College of Ophthalmologists similarly emphasizes early screening at 6 to 8 weeks, recommends genetic evaluation for all cases of bilateral congenital cataracts, and underscores interprofessional care. The World Health Organization Vision 2020 Initiative focuses on early detection and referral in resource-limited settings and advocates training primary health workers in red reflex testing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r100">[100]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s10" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Managing pediatric cataracts requires a coordinated effort among ophthalmologists, pediatricians, anesthesiologists, counselors, parents, and other family members. Treatment options may be medical or surgical.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Medical Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>Medical management includes amblyopia therapy, such as patching the better-seeing eye to stimulate use of the amblyopic eye or using atropine drops to blur vision in the dominant eye as an alternative.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r101">[101]</xref> Refractive correction is achieved through glasses or contact lenses to address residual refractive errors after surgery. Bifocals or multifocals may be needed for near-vision tasks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r102">[102]</xref>&#x000a0;Low vision aids, including magnifiers and telescopic lenses, can enhance visual function in children with significant visual impairment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r103">[103]</xref> In cases of partial or nonamblyogenic cataracts, 2.5% phenylephrine may be used to achieve mydriasis and permit vision through the nonopacified area. Optical iridectomy, though now obsolete, served a similar purpose by allowing light to pass through a clear zone of the lens.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r104">[104]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgery</bold>
</p>
        <p>Surgical indications include any visually significant lens opacity, cataracts associated with visual acuity of 20/60 or worse, cases where the optic disc is not visible on indirect ophthalmoscopy, and central cataracts measuring at least 3 mm in size. Other indications include posterior subcapsular and nuclear cataracts, bilateral involvement, and cataracts accompanied by strabismus or nystagmus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r105">[105]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r106">[106]</xref>&#x000a0;Before surgery, family members must be counseled about the expected visual prognosis, the available treatment options, and the importance of regular follow-up. Postoperative visual rehabilitation and adherence to amblyopia therapy, when indicated, are essential components of care.</p>
        <p>Surgery should be performed promptly for visually significant cataracts, ideally within a few weeks after birth, to prevent amblyopia. Surgical planning is more favorable for children older than 4, as ocular development is largely complete, and the risk of postoperative complications is lower. Unilateral cataracts are typically operated on between 4 and 6 weeks of age. For bilateral cases, the recommended interval is 6 to 8 weeks, with the second eye ideally&#x000a0;treated within 2 weeks of the first. In bilateral cataract surgery performed within the first year of life, long-term visual acuity at 5 years is not significantly affected by the exact timing of surgery. However, earlier intervention, preferably before 2 months, correlates with better visual outcomes&#x000a0;in unilateral cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r107">[107]</xref></p>
        <p>Despite these benefits, early cataract surgery&#x000a0;is associated with a higher risk of glaucoma, particularly in unilateral cases.&#x000a0;Thus, some surgeons may opt to delay the procedure slightly to balance visual and safety outcomes, accepting a modest reduction in visual acuity. General anesthesia with continuous monitoring of vital parameters is preferred for pediatric cataract surgery. A trained anesthesiology team and interprofessional&#x000a0;support are essential, given the unique physiological and surgical needs of children. The type of surgery and its associated challenges have evolved significantly. Modern techniques, such as phacoaspiration with IOL implantation, have largely replaced older methods, such as discission. In 1959, Choyce and Binkhorst were the first to implant a monocular IOL in a child.</p>
        <p>Pediatric cataract surgery differs considerably from adult surgery and presents distinct intraoperative challenges. Low scleral rigidity complicates incision construction and wound closure. A smaller globe size, shallow anterior chamber, and small pupil reduce surgical maneuverability. The highly elastic lens capsule, elevated positive intravitreal pressure, and risk of vitreous loss or expulsion of intraocular contents further increase procedural complexity.</p>
        <p>Primary IOL implantation offers several advantages, including immediate refractive correction, minimal optical aberration, a full visual field, and a reduced likelihood of amblyopia, strabismus, and nystagmus.&#x000a0;The&#x000a0;procedure also reduces the dependence on patient compliance for corrective devices. However, IOL implantation in children younger than 2 years remains controversial. Limited long-term data, frequent coexisting ocular abnormalities, high rates of deprivation amblyopia, and increased postoperative complications have historically discouraged early implantation.</p>
        <p>The Infant Aphakia Treatment Study reported a higher incidence of intraoperative and postoperative complications, as well as a greater need for additional surgeries, in children who underwent IOL implantation before 1 year of age.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r108">[108]</xref>&#x000a0;In contrast, recent data from the Swedish Pediatric Cataract Register suggest that primary bag-in-the-lens IOL implantation before 2 years of age is safe and effective. This technique was associated with low rates of visual axis opacity, supporting its routine use in Sweden.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r109">[109]</xref></p>
        <p>A consensus on the timing and technique of surgery with IOL implantation is as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Younger than 18 months to 2 years: Lens aspiration without IOL implantation, combined with posterior curvilinear capsulorhexis (PCCC) and limited anterior vitrectomy (LAV). The child remains aphakic and is managed with postoperative aphakic correction, followed by secondary IOL implantation at a later age.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>2 to 5 years: Lens aspiration with IOL implantation, along with PCCC and LAV.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Older than 8 years: Lens aspiration with IOL implantation and PCCC, without LAV. Alternatively, phacoaspiration with IOL implantation may be performed as in adult cataract surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r110">[110]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r111">[111]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>To reduce the risk of VAO, PCCC is recommended for children younger than 8 years.&#x000a0;LAV is advised for&#x000a0;patients younger than 5 years.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Results from recent studies have shown that posterior optic capture following PCCC without LAV yields comparable outcomes to conventional in-the-bag IOL implantation with PCCC and LAV in terms of posterior capsular opacification, complication rates, and refractive development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r112">[112]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r113">[113]</xref> However, posterior optic capture is technically demanding and has a steep learning curve.&#x000a0;The procedure requires precise sizing of both the anterior and posterior capsulorhexis and should be used cautiously in infants or eyes with posterior capsular defects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r114">[114]</xref></p>
        <p>Accurate IOL power calculation is crucial for achieving optimal refractive outcomes after surgery in children. However, this process poses significant challenges, particularly in accounting for the child's age and axial length. Biometry and keratometry measurements may vary depending on the instruments and techniques used. Studies have shown that the Sanders-Retzlaff-Kraff/Theoretical and Holladay 2 formulas yield the lowest predictive error among current IOL power formulas. Ongoing research is developing improved formulas to enhance the accuracy of IOL calculations in pediatric patients.</p>
        <p>"Emmetropisation" refers to the postnatal adjustment of&#x000a0;3 primary ocular parameters&#x02014;axial length, corneal curvature, and lenticular power&#x02014;toward adult values as the child matures. A progressive myopic shift is expected as axial length increases with ocular growth. Therefore, the initial refractive target after IOL implantation is hypermetropia. Undercorrection of IOL power based on age has been shown to improve postoperative refractive outcomes. Dahan and Drusedau recommended undercorrection by 20% in children younger than 2 years and by 10% in those aged 2 to 8 years. The consensus targets for residual refraction include&#x000a0;the following approximate values:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>1 to 2 years: +6 D</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>2 to 4 years: +5 D</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>4 to 5 years: +4 D</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>5 to 6 years: +3 D</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>6 to 7 years: +2 D</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Older than 14 years: plano target&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r115">[115]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r116">[116]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r117">[117]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r118">[118]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Results from a recent 15-year follow-up study reported that cataract surgery performed at or before 2 years of age resulted in significant refractive shifts. Median myopic or anisometropic changes ranged from -8 to -11 D, regardless of whether the child was aphakic or pseudophakic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r119">[119]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>National and International Guidelines</bold>
</p>
        <p>National and international guidelines provide comprehensive recommendations for the detection and management of pediatric cataracts. The AAP recommends performing red reflex examinations at every well-child visit beginning at birth, with immediate referral to a pediatric ophthalmologist if an abnormal red reflex is identified. The AAO emphasizes early detection and timely treatment, particularly in infants with visually significant cataracts. The AAO also recommends genetic counseling and appropriate genetic testing for families with a history of pediatric cataracts.</p>
        <p>Postoperative amblyopia therapy and regular follow-up are advised to monitor visual development and address complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r120">[120]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r121">[121]</xref> The WHO supports the integrated management of childhood illnesses, including screening for congenital conditions such as cataracts. The&#x000a0;WHO also advocates the development of national programs to prevent and manage childhood blindness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r122">[122]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Follow-Up Care</bold>
</p>
        <p>Follow-up care for pediatric cataracts involves several key components to ensure optimal outcomes. Regular ophthalmic evaluations are essential, with frequent follow-ups to monitor for complications in the immediate postoperative period. Long-term follow-up tracks visual development and addresses any refractive changes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r123">[123]</xref>&#x000a0;Developmental and educational supports, including occupational therapy, are important for promoting visual skill development and supporting activities of daily living. Special education services ensure appropriate accommodations for children with visual impairment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r124">[124]</xref>&#x000a0;Parental education and support are critical.</p>
        <p>Caregivers must receive clear instructions on postoperative care, including the administration of eye drops and recognition of potential complications. Emphasis is placed on adherence to amblyopia therapy and scheduled follow-up visits.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r125">[125]</xref>&#x000a0;Psychosocial support should also be provided, including access to counseling and support groups, to help families manage the emotional and psychological challenges associated with pediatric cataracts. This comprehensive and interprofessional approach, aligned with national and international guidelines on early detection, timely surgical intervention, effective postoperative care, and ongoing visual rehabilitation, is essential for optimizing visual outcomes and improving quality of life for affected children.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s11" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts typically present as a white, opaque corneal lens, often first noticed by parents. Other potential&#x000a0;etiologies must be excluded before diagnosing a pediatric cataract as the cause of a white pupillary reflex (ie, leukocoria). These conditions include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Corneal abnormalities (eg, corneal opacity)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lens abnormalities (eg, cataracts)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Vitreous causes (eg, vitreous hemorrhage, persistent hyperplastic primary vitreous)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Retinal disorders (eg, Coats disease, retinoblastoma, familial exudative vitreoretinopathy, retinal detachment, retinopathy of prematurity, and coloboma)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tumors such as medulloepithelioma and retinal astrocytoma&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r126">[126]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r127">[127]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Cataracts may occur in isolation or association with other causes of leukocoria. The differential diagnosis&#x000a0;may be refined based on the clinical and family histories and a comprehensive ophthalmic examination.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r128">[128]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r129">[129]</xref>&#x000a0;Other potential causes of lens opacity must also be considered, including the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>High-risk&#x000a0;leukocoria&#x000a0;conditions</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Retinoblastoma (most important)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r130">[130]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Coats disease</p></list-item><list-item><p>Persistent fetal vasculature</p></list-item><list-item><p>Retinal detachment&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r131">[131]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Toxocariasis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Optic nerve hypoplasia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Retinal hemorrhages (eg, shaken baby syndrome)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Intraocular inflammation (uveitis)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r132">[132]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Hyphema</p></list-item><list-item><p>Retinal vein occlusion</p></list-item><list-item><p>Severe uveitis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r133">[133]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Congenital or developmental eye disorders</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Congenital glaucoma&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r134">[134]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Peters anomaly</p></list-item><list-item><p>Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Aniridia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Coloboma&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r135">[135]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Albinism</p></list-item><list-item><p>Blue cone monochromacy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Congenital stationary night blindness</p></list-item><list-item><p>Persistent fetal vasculature</p></list-item><list-item><p>Retinal dysplasia</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Syndromic&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;genetic disorders with ocular involvement</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Marfan syndrome&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r136">[136]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Stickler syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bardet-Biedl syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Joubert syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Von Hippel-Lindau disease</p></list-item><list-item><p>Wolfram syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Langerhans cell histiocytosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Oculocerebrorenal syndrome (Lowe syndrome)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Alport syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Usher syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>WAGR (Wilms tumor, aniridia, genitourinary anomalies, developmental delays)&#x000a0;syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ehlers-Danlos syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ataxia-telangiectasia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Neurofibromatosis type 1</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Metabolic&#x000a0;or&#x000a0;systemic disorders affecting the wye</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Lowe syndrome&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r137">[137]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Galactosemia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Diabetes mellitus</p></list-item><list-item><p>Familial hypercholesterolemia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Fabry disease</p></list-item><list-item><p>Niemann-Pick disease</p></list-item><list-item><p>Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypocalcemia</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Infectious causes</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Congenital rubella syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cytomagalovirus</p></list-item><list-item><p>Toxoplasmosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Herpes simplex virus infection&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r138">[138]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Congenital varicella syndrome</p></list-item><list-item><p>Tuberculosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Congenital Zika syndrome</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Retinal or macular dystrophies</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Retinitis pigmentosa&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r139">[139]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>Best disease (Best vitelliform macular dystrophy)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Choroideremia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bietti crystalline dystrophy</p></list-item><list-item><p>Batten disease (neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis)</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Corneal conditions (not true cataracts)</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Sclerocornea</p></list-item><list-item><p>Corneal opacity from metabolic diseases&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r140">[140]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Vitreous causes</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Vitreous hemorrhage</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Trauma-related causes</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Lens subluxation</p></list-item><list-item><p>Vitreous hemorrhage</p></list-item><list-item><p>Retinal detachment</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Genetic conditions</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Albinism</p></list-item><list-item><p>Aniridia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Foveal hypoplasia&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r141">[141]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>A thorough clinical investigation with the appropriate diagnostic modalities can guide management.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Distinguishing Between Leukocoria Mimics and Cataracts</bold>
</p>
        <p>Leukocoria in children requires ruling out pediatric cataract mimics with life-threatening potential.&#x000a0;Key mimics, clinical clues, and rule-out methods are explained below. Corneal opacities, including sclerocornea, Peters anomaly, corneal dystrophies, and congenital glaucoma-related edema, may mimic pediatric cataracts by producing a white pupillary reflex. Distinguishing features include corneal clouding, enlarged corneal diameter as seen in glaucoma, and Haab striae, which&#x000a0;may be evaluated through slit-lamp examination, corneal diameter measurement, and IOP testing. Persistent fetal vasculature may also present with leukocoria resembling cataract, with associated microphthalmia, a stalk behind the lens, and a retrolental membrane.&#x000a0;Diagnosis is confirmed using B-scan ultrasonography and dilated fundus examination.</p>
        <p>Retinoblastoma, a life-threatening condition, may be mistaken for cataract due to leukocoria but is differentiated by strabismus and the presence of a calcified intraocular mass, requiring urgent evaluation with ultrasound, magnetic resonance imaging, or computed tomography. Coats disease typically presents with unilateral leukocoria, accompanied by telangiectatic retinal vessels and exudation, which&#x000a0;may be identified through fundus examination and fluorescein angiography. Retinal detachment, whether congenital or traumatic, may mimic a pediatric cataract by producing a white reflex or a reduced red reflex. Key distinguishing features include an elevated or folded retinal surface, which can be confirmed by B-scan ultrasonography. Toxocariasis typically presents with a white pupillary reflex; however, focal granuloma and vitreous inflammation help distinguish it, with enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA), fundus examination, and ultrasound serving as confirmatory tools.</p>
        <p>Coloboma of the lens or retina may produce an irregular pupillary or retinal reflex resembling a cataract.&#x000a0;Inferonasal defects and possible associated anomalies are identified through dilated examination and&#x000a0;optical coherence tomography (OCT).&#x000a0;Aniridia&#x000a0;may reduce the red reflex due to foveal hypoplasia and is associated with iris deficiency and nystagmus; diagnosis is confirmed by slit-lamp examination and <italic toggle="yes">PAX6</italic> genetic testing. Albinism may present with a reduced red reflex or light scattering, resembling a mild cataract.&#x000a0;Transillumination defects and nystagmus, confirmed with OCT demonstrating foveal hypoplasia, distinguish the condition. Vitreous hemorrhage opacifies the visual axis, similar to a cataract, and is suspected from a history of trauma and a hazy pupil, with B-scan ultrasonography aiding diagnosis.</p>
        <p>Vitreous hemorrhage can mimic a pediatric cataract by opacifying the visual axis. A history of trauma and a hazy pupil suggest the diagnosis, which is confirmed with B-scan ultrasonography. Intraocular foreign bodies, though rare in infants, typically present as an opacity behind the lens and are suspected on the basis of a history of trauma and the presence of a metallic echo.&#x000a0;Radiography or CT of the orbit can confirm the finding. Uveitis with posterior synechiae or pupillary membranes may present as pupillary opacities resembling cataract. Distinguishing features include cells, flare, and keratic precipitates, and the condition can be diagnosed by slit-lamp examination and inflammatory laboratory tests.</p>
        <p>Congenital glaucoma produces corneal edema that creates a cloudy pupil, accompanied by excessive tearing, photophobia, and enlarged corneal diameter, confirmed by IOP measurement and corneal sizing. High myopia in infants may reduce the foveal reflex, mimicking cataract on screening.&#x000a0;Long axial length and myopic streaks are characteristic of this condition, as confirmed by refraction and axial length measurement. A mature, intumescent lens can produce a white reflex in advanced stages, with lens enlargement apparent on slit-lamp examination. Certain syndromes, such as Costello or Pallister-Killian syndrome, can cause refractive lens changes that mimic cataract, identifiable through facial dysmorphism, systemic findings, and genetic evaluation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r142">[142]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s12" sec-type="Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials">
        <title>Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials</title>
        <p>Pediatric cataract is primarily a surgical, developmental, and genetic condition rather than a pharmacologic disease. The evidence base derives from large multicenter cohort studies, population-based trials, genetic and metabolic research, and long-term outcome studies of surgical techniques. International guidelines from AAPOS, AAO, the Royal College, and WHO also inform management. A limited number of ongoing interventional trials focus on IOL design, amblyopia therapy, and postoperative care. Evidence does not support&#x000a0;radiation therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r143">[143]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Key Pertinent Studies Influencing Current Practice</bold>
</p>
        <p>Key studies have shaped current pediatric cataract management. The IATS, a landmark multicenter randomized controlled trial, compared primary IOL implantation with aphakia corrected using contact lenses in infants aged 1&#x000a0;to 6 months with unilateral congenital cataract. The study found no significant difference in visual acuity at 4.5 years.&#x000a0;However, primary IOL implantation in infants younger than 6 months was associated with higher complication and reoperation rates, whereas aphakia with contact lenses led to fewer adverse events. These results prompted AAPOS and AAO to recommend caution when implanting IOLs in infants younger than 6 months, emphasizing individualized decision-making.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r144">[144]</xref></p>
        <p>Observational cohort studies on early surgery timing consistently demonstrate that dense bilateral cataracts should ideally be removed by 6&#x000a0;to 8 weeks of age, while unilateral cataracts should typically be excised by 4&#x000a0;to 6 weeks. Delays beyond 10 weeks are associated with severe amblyopia, consistent with the &#x0201c;sensitive period&#x0201d; of visual development, as shown by Birch, Lambert, and colleagues.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r145">[145]</xref>&#x000a0;Longitudinal studies on postoperative glaucoma, including reports from Wang, Beck, and the IATS glaucoma follow-up, indicate that 30% to 40% of children develop glaucoma after pediatric cataract surgery. The risk is higher in infants operated on before 6 months, in eyes with microphthalmia, persistent fetal vasculature, or juvenile-onset open-angle glaucoma patterns, underscoring the necessity for lifelong IOP monitoring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r146">[146]</xref></p>
        <p>Although not specific to cataract management, amblyopia treatment trials from the Amblyopia Treatment Studies conducted by the Pediatric Eye Disease Investigator Group provide the evidence base for postoperative visual rehabilitation. These studies inform full-time versus part-time patching schedules, atropine penalization, and compliance strategies, establishing the current protocols for postcataract amblyopia therapy. Genetic sequencing studies, including work by Rahi, Hejtmancik, and collaborators, have identified more than 100 genes associated with congenital cataracts, with next-generation sequencing achieving a diagnostic yield of 40% to 60%. These findings support the international recommendation for genetic testing in bilateral cataracts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r147">[147]</xref></p>
        <p>Epidemiological studies from India, Brazil, and Africa show that rubella remains a significant cause of congenital cataract in regions with incomplete vaccination coverage. Congenital rubella cataracts are often accompanied by deafness and cardiac anomalies, reinforcing the importance of maternal vaccination programs. The WHO continues to list congenital cataract as a priority condition for avoidable blindness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r148">[148]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Studies on Surgical Techniques and Outcomes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Studies on surgical techniques and outcomes further inform contemporary pediatric cataract management. Investigations by Wilson, Trivedi, Vasavada, and Dahan demonstrate that combining posterior capsulotomy with anterior vitrectomy in children younger than 6 years significantly reduces VAO and decreases the need for secondary interventions, establishing this combined approach as a standard technique worldwide in this age group.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r149">[149]</xref>&#x000a0;Multiple studies evaluating IOL power calculation highlight the challenges unique to pediatric eyes. Formulas such as&#x000a0;Sanders-Retzlaff-Kraff/Theoretical and Holladay 1 are commonly used in older children, while Holladay 2 and Barrett Universal II are emerging as more accurate options in pediatric populations. Large corneal curvature and shorter axial length complicate refractive prediction, leading many surgeons to intentionally leave residual undercorrection to accommodate the anticipated myopic shift associated with rapid ocular growth.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r150">[150]</xref></p>
        <p>Outcomes of contact lens correction in aphakic children have also been examined. Comparative studies of silicone elastomer lenses and rigid gas-permeable lenses show good visual outcomes when compliance is excellent.&#x000a0;However, a high caregiver burden is frequently reported, and cost remains a critical consideration in resource-limited settings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r151">[151]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Ongoing Clinical Trials and Research Areas</bold>
</p>
        <p>Ongoing research continues to refine pediatric cataract management across surgical, genetic, and rehabilitative domains. Clinical trials evaluating next-generation adjustable IOLs, including light-adjustable and modular types, focus on the feasibility of postoperative refractive adjustment, long-term safety and biocompatibility, and the potential to reduce long-term anisometropia. Gene therapy trials remain in early-phase investigation, targeting gene replacement for crystallin mutations and correction of connexin-related cataract pathways.&#x000a0;These approaches remain experimental and have not yet entered clinical practice.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r152">[152]</xref></p>
        <p>Pharmacologic strategies aimed at preventing secondary cataract formation after surgery are under investigation,&#x000a0;including agents such as mammalian target of rapamycin inhibitors, transforming growth factor&#x02013;beta inhibitors, and capsular bag&#x02013;surface modifying compounds, with the primary goal of reducing posterior capsule opacification.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r153">[153]</xref> Advances in visual rehabilitation technology are also being studied, including digital amblyopia therapy, virtual reality&#x02013;based alternatives to patching, and artificial intelligence&#x02013;driven compliance monitoring, with particular relevance to postoperative unilateral cataract.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r154">[154]</xref>&#x000a0;Long-term neurodevelopmental outcome studies follow children after cataract surgery to assess cognitive development, binocular vision potential, and the comparative impact of early versus delayed intervention. Findings from these studies continue to inform and refine evidence-based guidelines regarding the optimal timing of surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r155">[155]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s13" sec-type="Treatment Planning ">
        <title>Treatment Planning </title>
        <p>Treatment planning for pediatric cataract is complex and must account for timing, laterality, cataract type, ocular comorbidities, systemic disorders, and long-term visual rehabilitation needs. Given the heightened sensitivity of visual development in early childhood, pediatric cataract management is time-critical and interprofessional, requiring coordination among pediatric ophthalmologists, anesthesiologists, optometrists, geneticists, and pediatricians. This planning is guided by clearly defined objectives that prioritize visual development and long-term ocular health. Primary goals include early restoration of a clear visual axis, prevention or treatment of amblyopia, achievement of appropriate refractive correction, management of coexisting ocular or systemic conditions, and establishment of lifelong follow-up to monitor for complications such as glaucoma.</p>
        <p>The visual significance of the cataract determines the necessity for treatment. Intervention is indicated when lens opacity obscures the visual axis, a central cataract measures at least 3 mm, or posterior cataracts involve the visual axis and impair central vision. Additional indications include strabismus or nystagmus and rapid cataract progression. According to AAPOS guidelines, any visually significant cataract identified during infancy warrants urgent referral and evaluation for surgical management. The timing of surgery is a critical component of treatment planning because the developing visual system is highly susceptible to deprivation. Delayed intervention during sensitive periods of visual maturation substantially increases the risk of irreversible amblyopia, underscoring the importance of prompt, age-appropriate surgical decision-making.</p>
        <p>Surgical timing in pediatric cataract is determined by laterality, density, etiology, and the risk of visual deprivation. Dense unilateral congenital cataracts require surgical removal at approximately 4 to 6 weeks of age, while dense bilateral congenital cataracts are optimally treated between 6 and 8 weeks of age to minimize amblyogenic risk. Partial or developmental cataracts are managed based on visual axis clarity, rate of progression, and measurable visual function rather than strict age thresholds. Traumatic cataracts require individualized timing, guided by the degree of inflammation, capsular integrity, and the risk of lens-induced glaucoma. Delays beyond 10 to 12 weeks of age are associated with permanent visual deprivation and poor long-term visual outcomes.</p>
        <p>Preoperative planning for pediatric cataract surgery requires a detailed ocular evaluation, precise biometric assessment, and careful procedural selection to minimize complications and optimize long-term visual outcomes.&#x000a0;Comprehensive ocular assessment includes a slit-lamp examination to evaluate lens opacity and to identify associated anomalies, such as posterior synechiae, PFV, aniridia, coloboma, and microphthalmia. IOP measurement is essential to detect preexisting or secondary glaucoma, which may influence surgical timing and technique.</p>
        <p>Biometric evaluation is mandatory when IOL implantation is under consideration. Required measurements include axial length, keratometry, and anterior chamber depth. These parameters are critical for IOL power calculation and surgical planning, particularly in eyes with abnormal growth patterns. Several factors influence the decision to implant an IOL. Age represents the most critical determinant, given the rapid ocular growth in infancy. Ocular size must be carefully assessed, as microphthalmia is associated with an increased risk of intraoperative and postoperative complications. The presence of ocular comorbidities, including&#x000a0;persistent fetal vasculature or uveitis, may contraindicate primary implantation. Surgeon experience and family capacity to maintain contact lens therapy also influence decision-making.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r156">[156]</xref>&#x000a0;Evidence-based guidance from the IATS recommends avoiding primary IOL implantation in infants younger than 6 months. Between 6 months and 2 years of age, the decision should be individualized. Beyond 2 years of age, primary IOL implantation is considered standard practice.</p>
        <p>The preferred&#x000a0;surgical approach for pediatric cataract includes lens aspiration followed by anterior capsulotomy, performed manually or using a vitrector. Complete irrigation&#x02013;aspiration of lens material is required. Posterior capsulotomy with anterior vitrectomy is routinely performed and is mandatory in children 6 years of age or younger to reduce the risk of&#x000a0;posterior capsule opacification (PCO). IOL implantation is performed when indicated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r157">[157]</xref></p>
        <p>Preferred IOLs include single-piece acrylic lenses, with 3-piece designs reserved for cases requiring sulcus placement. Refractive targeting typically favors mild hyperopia in younger children to compensate for the anticipated myopic shift associated with ocular growth. Contraindications to IOL implantation include severe microphthalmia, active uveitis, aniridia with inadequate capsular support, and uncontrolled glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r158">[158]</xref></p>
        <p>Immediate postoperative management of pediatric cataract involves anti-inflammatory therapy with frequent topical steroids and cycloplegics to control inflammation and improve comfort. Topical broad-spectrum antibiotics are administered for 1 week to prevent infection, and IOP monitoring is essential given the risk of early postoperative glaucoma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r159">[159]</xref> Optical rehabilitation depends on lens status: aphakic eyes require contact lenses or aphakic spectacles, whereas pseudophakic eyes need age-appropriate spectacles for residual refractive error. Occlusion therapy is indicated for unilateral cataracts, with frequency and duration guided by the ATS, and atropine penalization may serve as an alternative in selected cases.</p>
        <p>Long-term management of pediatric cataract requires a structured follow-up schedule, with weekly or biweekly visits initially, monthly assessments during the first year, and evaluations every 3 to 6 months thereafter. Annual reviews continue lifelong to monitor for glaucoma, PCO, refractive changes, and secondary membrane formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r160">[160]</xref> Complication surveillance includes early- and late-onset glaucoma, visual axis opacity (VAO), IOL decentration, strabismus, and recurrence of amblyopia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r161">[161]</xref></p>
        <p>Interprofessional coordination includes pediatricians to evaluate associated systemic disease; geneticists to assess bilateral or syndromic cataracts and a positive family history; and low-vision specialists to manage visual rehabilitation in children with ocular anomalies, such as microphthalmia or retinal disease. Family counseling emphasizes the lifelong nature of follow-up, the importance of patching and optical correction, realistic visual outcomes, and identification of socioeconomic factors that may affect compliance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r162">[162]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r163">[163]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s14" sec-type="Toxicity and Adverse Effect Management">
        <title>Toxicity and Adverse Effect Management</title>
        <p>Although systemic chemotherapy or radiation is not used to treat pediatric cataract, "toxicity and adverse effect management" refers to complications related to surgical intervention, adverse effects of postoperative medications, issues arising from implantable devices such as IOL, and long-term consequences of early ocular surgery in infants. This section outlines potential adverse events and presents evidence-based strategies for their management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r164">[164]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Toxicities and Adverse Effects</bold>
</p>
        <p>Surgical toxicities and adverse effects in pediatric cataract include intraoperative complications such as posterior capsule rupture caused by excessive aspiration, weak capsule, or persistent fetal vasculature (PFV),&#x000a0;managed with anterior vitrectomy and modified IOL placement if necessary. Iris trauma, often resulting from a small pupil or synechiae, is addressed using iris hooks or sphincterotomy and postoperative topical steroids. Zonular dialysis, due to congenital weakness or trauma, requires a capsular tension ring or modified IOL fixation. Hyphema from instrumental trauma is managed with observation, topical steroids, and, in rare cases, anterior chamber washout.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Postoperative Toxicities and Their Management</bold>
</p>
        <p>Postoperative toxicities after pediatric cataract surgery encompass inflammation, secondary glaucoma, VAO, infection, and IOL-related complications. Children exhibit robust postoperative inflammation, characterized by fibrinous uveitis, posterior synechiae, and membrane formation, and are managed with intensive topical steroids (initially every 1&#x02013;2 hours), cycloplegics to prevent synechiae, subconjunctival steroids in severe cases, and adjunctive nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r165">[165]</xref>&#x000a0;Secondary glaucoma affects 15% to 40% of children postoperatively, with the highest risk when surgery occurs before 6 months of age, presenting as open-angle, angle-closure, or pupillary block glaucoma. Lifelong IOP surveillance, age-appropriate gonioscopy, and optic nerve evaluation are essential. Management includes topical IOP-lowering agents (eg, &#x003b2;-blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors; avoid prostaglandins in infants) and surgical interventions, such as trabeculotomy, goniotomy, or valve implantation, for refractory cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r166">[166]</xref></p>
        <p>VAO, the most common adverse outcome in young children, arises from lens epithelial cell proliferation and is prevented by primary posterior capsulotomy combined with anterior vitrectomy in children&#x000a0;younger than 6. Secondary treatment includes&#x000a0;yttrium aluminum garnet capsulotomy in older children or surgical membranectomy if laser application is not feasible.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r167">[167]</xref> Endophthalmitis is rare but sight-threatening, with risk factors including wound leak or intraoperative contamination.&#x000a0;Management requires immediate intravitreal antibiotics, vitrectomy in severe cases, and prophylaxis with intracameral and postoperative topical antibiotics.</p>
        <p>IOL-related toxicities include decentration or dislocation, particularly in cases of zonular weakness, as seen in Marfan syndrome or PFV, and are managed by repositioning, scleral fixation, or IOL exchange. Refractive surprise, or myopic shift, results from rapid eye growth and is managed with regular refraction, spectacles, contact lenses, or adjustment of the IOL target. Rarely,&#x000a0;uveitis&#x02013;glaucoma&#x02013;hyphema syndrome can develop from iris chafing and is managed with IOL repositioning or exchange, along with steroid therapy.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Medication-Related Toxicities</bold>
</p>
        <p>Medication-related toxicities in pediatric cataract care include steroid-induced glaucoma, atropine-related systemic effects, and contact lens&#x02013;related complications in aphakia. Children are often steroid responders, necessitating IOP measurement at every visit while on steroids. Management involves gradual tapering, switching to weaker agents such as loteprednol, and adding IOP-lowering drops when indicated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r168">[168]</xref> Atropine, used for amblyopia therapy and postoperative comfort, may cause flushing, fever, tachycardia, and central nervous system effects in small infants. Management requires the minimal effective dose and parental education on signs of systemic toxicity. Use of aphakic contact lenses can lead to keratitis, hypoxia, and corneal neovascularization.&#x000a0;Strict hygiene, high oxygen-permeability lenses, and regular corneal evaluation mitigate these risks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r169">[169]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Long-Term Adverse Outcomes</bold>
</p>
        <p>Amblyopia typically develops in nearly all cases of unilateral cataract unless the cataract is aggressively treated. Management includes patching in accordance with ATS protocols, atropine penalization when appropriate, and consistent follow-up to detect recurrence. Strabismus may arise from visual deprivation or postoperative ocular imbalance. Management consists of optical correction, amblyopia therapy, and strabismus surgery once ocular alignment stabilizes. Late-onset glaucoma can develop years to decades after surgery. Lifelong follow-up is essential, with early intervention using IOP-lowering medications or surgical procedures when indicated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r170">[170]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Family Education as Part of Toxicity Prevention</bold>
</p>
        <p>Family education plays a critical role in preventing treatment-related toxicities. Emphasis should be placed on strict adherence to eye drops, patching regimens, and scheduled follow-up visits. Families must be informed of signs of infection, including ocular pain, redness, and photophobia. Education should also address the long-term risk of glaucoma and provide instruction on proper contact lens hygiene for aphakic infants.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s15" sec-type="Staging">
        <title>Staging</title>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts may be staged based on morphology, location, and visual impact. Staging provides a framework for guiding appropriate treatment and management strategies. The criteria commonly used for staging are summarized below.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Morphological Classification</bold>
</p>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts&#x000a0;may&#x000a0;be classified by the location and pattern of lens opacities. Morphological classification aids in assessing severity and predicting the potential impact on visual development.&#x000a0;Each morphological type presents unique clinical considerations. Awareness of these differences ensures more tailored treatment and follow-up strategies.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Nuclear cataract:</bold> Located in the central nucleus of the lens; often congenital and can significantly impair vision if dense</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cortical cataract:</bold> Affects the cortex, or outer layer, of the lens; may progress slowly, with the degree of visual impairment depending on the extent and location of the opacities</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Posterior subcapsular cataract:</bold> Positioned at the back of the lens, just&#x000a0;anterior to the posterior capsule; often causes glare and difficulty with bright light,&#x000a0;markedly affecting near vision</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Lamellar (zonular) cataract:</bold> Involves a specific layer or zone of the lens (see <bold>Image</bold>. Pediatric Cataract, Zonular Type); dense, centrally located opacities can cause substantial visual impairment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Anterior polar cataract:&#x000a0;</bold>Located on the anterior surface of the lens capsule; typically small and may not&#x000a0;considerably&#x000a0;impair vision unless&#x000a0;the cataract&#x000a0;enlarges&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r171">[171]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Severity Based on Density and Extent</bold>
</p>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts&#x000a0;may also be staged based on severity, which helps guide clinical decisions. Classification into mild, moderate, or severe reflects the degree of visual impact and informs the timing of treatment.&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Mild cataract:</bold> Small lens opacities that do not&#x000a0;substantially&#x000a0;interfere with vision; managed conservatively with regular monitoring</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Moderate cataract:</bold> More extensive opacities that cause noticeable visual impairment; may require surgical intervention if vision is functionally compromised</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Severe cataract:</bold> Dense opacities that markedly impair vision or cause blindness; prompt surgical management is typically necessary to restore vision and reduce the risk of amblyopia&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r172">[172]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Effective management of pediatric cataracts depends on accurate assessment of severity. Early intervention in moderate-to-severe cases is critical for optimal visual outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Impact on Vision</bold>
</p>
        <p>Assessing the functional impact of a cataract on a child&#x02019;s vision is crucial. This classification supports early identification of cases requiring surgical management versus observation.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Visually insignificant cataract:</bold> Lens opacity that does not&#x000a0;strikingly impair vision or causes only minor visual disturbances. Regular follow-up is necessary to monitor for progression.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Visually significant cataract:&#x000a0;</bold>Leads to marked visual impairment that affects daily function and visual development. Requires timely surgical intervention to prevent amblyopia and optimize visual outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r173">[173]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Timely identification of visually significant cataracts ensures appropriate surgical planning and supports better developmental outcomes. Nonsignificant cataracts should still be monitored for possible progression.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Location Relative to the Visual Axis</bold>
</p>
        <p>The location of a pediatric cataract within the lens directly influences visual development and the severity of visual disturbance. Cataracts are commonly classified as central or peripheral based on their location.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Central cataract:</bold> Situated along the central visual axis; more likely to interfere with vision and visual development, often necessitating early intervention</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Peripheral cataract:</bold> Located outside the central visual axis; may not significantly impair vision unless the cataract&#x000a0;progresses centrally</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>This classification assists clinicians in prioritizing cases for surgical referral; central cataracts pose a higher risk and should be addressed more urgently</p>
        <p>
<bold>Laterality</bold>
</p>
        <p>The distinction between unilateral and bilateral cataracts guides prognosis and therapeutic approach. Early identification is essential to minimize the risk of long-term visual impairment.&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Unilateral cataract:</bold> Involves only&#x000a0;1 eye; carries a higher risk of amblyopia due to unequal visual input, necessitating early diagnosis and prompt intervention</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Bilateral cataracts:</bold> Involve both eyes; can cause symmetrical visual impairment and typically require coordinated surgical and rehabilitative management&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r174">[174]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Laterality not only affects the severity of visual loss but also determines surgical timing and rehabilitation. Tailored strategies are essential for optimal outcomes across all types. Staging pediatric cataracts involves evaluating the morphology, severity, location, and visual impact. This comprehensive assessment informs treatment planning and supports optimal visual outcomes and development. Regular monitoring and timely intervention are essential for effective management.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s16" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Numerous factors influence the final visual outcome in children with pediatric cataracts. Visually significant cataracts produce blurred retinal images, which disrupt the development of visual pathways and cortical connections in the occipital lobe. With improved understanding and surgical techniques, early removal of visually significant cataracts is now recommended to prevent sensory deprivation amblyopia. Unilateral cataracts are more amblyogenic than bilateral cases, underscoring the importance of early surgery within the first few weeks to months of life.</p>
        <p>The timing of diagnosis plays a critical role in determining visual prognosis&#x02014;the earlier the diagnosis and treatment, the better the outcome. Ocular conditions such as microcornea, corneal opacities, glaucoma, intraocular inflammation, posterior segment abnormalities, and ocular motility disorders (eg, unsteady fixation, strabismus, or nystagmus) are associated with poorer postsurgical outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r175">[175]</xref>&#x000a0;The prognosis of pediatric cataracts depends largely on the timing of diagnosis and intervention, the presence of systemic or genetic conditions, and the effective management of complications. Early detection and prompt treatment are essential for optimizing long-term visual outcomes.</p>
        <p>Children diagnosed and treated within the first few weeks to months of life generally have a better prognosis. Early intervention prevents amblyopia and supports normal visual development. In contrast, delays in diagnosis and treatment can lead to permanent visual impairment, including amblyopia and associated developmental deficits, underscoring the importance of timely detection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r176">[176]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients&#x000a0;with isolated cataracts, ie, those not associated with ocular or systemic abnormalities, tend to have a more favorable prognosis when treated early. Cataracts linked to genetic syndromes or metabolic disorders present additional management challenges and often carry a more guarded prognosis due to the presence of concurrent health issues.</p>
        <p>Modern surgical techniques, including&#x000a0;IOL&#x000a0;implantation, have markedly improved outcomes in pediatric cataract management. Successful cataract extraction, followed by effective visual rehabilitation, frequently results in favorable visual outcomes. Postoperative care plays a crucial role.&#x000a0;Proper management of complications such as PCO and secondary glaucoma, along with regular follow-up and adherence to treatment, considerably&#x000a0;enhances long-term prognosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r177">[177]</xref></p>
        <p>Corrective lenses, whether glasses or contact lenses, along with amblyopia therapy, such as occlusion of the better-seeing eye, are essential components of visual rehabilitation. Consistent use of these interventions is critical for optimal visual development. Ongoing support through occupational therapy and visual skills training can further improve outcomes, particularly in children with developmental delays secondary to visual impairment.</p>
        <p>Continued surveillance is required to detect and manage late-onset complications such as glaucoma or retinal detachment, which may adversely affect long-term prognosis. Regular ophthalmic evaluations throughout childhood and adolescence enable timely identification and management of these conditions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r178">[178]</xref>&#x000a0;Visual outcomes vary widely across patients. Prognosis&#x000a0;depends on the type and severity of the cataract, the child's general health, the presence of coexisting ocular or systemic abnormalities, and the family&#x02019;s adherence to postoperative care and rehabilitation strategies.</p>
        <p>Overall, the prognosis for children with pediatric cataracts is favorable when early detection, timely intervention, and comprehensive postoperative care are provided. Advances in surgical techniques and visual rehabilitation have significantly improved outcomes, enabling many children to attain good vision and normal functional development. Continued vigilance remains essential to manage potential complications and preserve long-term visual health.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s17" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>As with other intraocular procedures, pediatric cataract surgery carries inherent risks that may necessitate further treatment.&#x000a0;These complications can compromise visual outcomes if not promptly identified and managed. Visual axis opacity (VAO) is a potential cause of amblyopia following cataract surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r179">[179]</xref>&#x000a0;The condition is inevitable if the posterior capsule is left intact.&#x000a0;VAO arises from the proliferation, migration, and metaplasia of lens epithelial cells extending from the equator to the posterior capsule. Even after a well-executed posterior capsulotomy, secondary membranes and media opacities may still form. An intact hyaloid face can serve as a scaffold for epithelial cell migration and transformation.</p>
        <p>One of the most influential factors in VAO development is the patient's age at the time of surgery. Other contributing factors include the type of cataract, the presence of associated ocular abnormalities (eg, PHPV, uveitis), aphakic or pseudophakic status, IOL type, the size of the capsular opening, and the completeness of cortical cleanup. Adequate posterior capsulotomy, with or without vitrectomy, is essential to prevent VAO. In-the-bag IOL implantation further reduces the risk. If the child is cooperative, Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy may be used to clear the opacification. Surgical intervention, such as membranectomy, may be necessary to restore visual axis clarity in cases of dense or thick VAO.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r180">[180]</xref></p>
        <p>Glaucoma develops in 10% to 25% of cases following pediatric cataract surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r181">[181]</xref> Open-angle glaucoma is more common, although pupillary block glaucoma can also occur. Medical therapy is typically the initial approach.&#x000a0;However, surgical management becomes necessary in refractory cases. Surgical options include trabeculectomy, implantation of glaucoma drainage devices, and cyclodestructive procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r182">[182]</xref></p>
        <p>Postoperative anterior uveitis&#x000a0;is more common in children than in adults due to heightened tissue reactivity. No-touch surgical techniques and in-the-bag IOL fixation help reduce the incidence of this condition. Frequent postoperative administration of topical steroids or posterior sub-Tenon injection of triamcinolone acetonide may be necessary. Dense fibrinous exudates and postoperative miosis occur in some cases. Mydriatic agents, such as atropine or subconjunctival injection of mydricaine, may be used to prevent synechiae formation and achieve adequate mydriasis.</p>
        <p>Postoperative IOL-related complications may include surface deposits, synechiae, pupillary capture, opacification, and decentration.&#x000a0;Additional IOL-related issues may involve incorrect IOL power calculation, dislocation, or intraocular inflammation. These complications highlight the importance of accurate IOL power estimation and meticulous surgical technique. Surgical repositioning or replacement may be required in cases of IOL dislocation or significant malfunction.</p>
        <p>Amblyopia is a developmental condition characterized by reduced visual acuity, often due to unequal visual input. This condition frequently arises in children with cataracts, particularly when unilateral or dense. Early diagnosis and intervention are essential to prevent long-term visual impairment. Treatment strategies include occlusion therapy (patching of the dominant eye), pharmacologic penalization with atropine, and refractive correction using glasses or contact lenses.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r183">[183]</xref></p>
        <p>PCO is the most common postoperative complication following pediatric cataract surgery. The lens capsule becomes cloudy, resulting in decreased visual acuity. Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy is typically performed to restore a clear visual axis and improve vision.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r184">[184]</xref>&#x000a0;Strabismus may develop in children with cataracts due to unequal visual input to the brain, which disrupts binocular vision and leads to ocular misalignment. Management options include corrective glasses, patching, orthoptic exercises,&#x000a0;and surgical realignment of the extraocular muscles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r185">[185]</xref></p>
        <p>Postoperative inflammation and infection, including endophthalmitis, are serious complications of cataract surgery in children. Prophylactic antibiotics and anti-inflammatory agents are administered postoperatively to minimize risk. Rapid recognition and aggressive treatment of infection are essential to prevent irreversible vision loss.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r186">[186]</xref>&#x000a0;Retinal detachment, though rare, is a potentially sight-threatening complication characterized by the separation of the neurosensory retina from the underlying retinal pigment epithelium. Immediate surgical repair is necessary to reattach the retina and preserve visual function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r187">[187]</xref></p>
        <p>Postoperative refractive errors, such as myopia&#x000a0;(nearsightedness)&#x000a0;and hyperopia&#x000a0;(farsightedness), may occur after pediatric cataract surgery and may affect visual acuity. Corrective lenses, ie, glasses or contact lenses, are prescribed to address these errors and achieve optimal visual outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r188">[188]</xref>&#x000a0;Phthisis bulbi is a condition in which the eye becomes shrunken and nonfunctional due to severe intraocular damage or chronic inflammation. Preventive strategies include timely and appropriate management of postoperative complications. Enucleation may be necessary in advanced or painful cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r189">[189]</xref></p>
        <p>Corneal opacification may develop as a result of chronic inflammation or infection, reducing corneal transparency and impairing vision. Continuous monitoring and aggressive management of underlying causes are essential. Corneal transplantation may be required&#x000a0;to restore visual function in advanced cases.</p>
        <p>Other complications may include cystoid macular edema, retinal detachment, endophthalmitis, heterochromia, corneal decompensation, bullous keratopathy, astigmatism, and unexpected postoperative refractive outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r190">[190]</xref>&#x000a0;Monitoring and managing&#x000a0;morbidities linked to pediatric cataracts are essential for achieving optimal visual outcomes. An interprofessional approach that emphasizes regular follow-up, timely intervention, and comprehensive care is crucial for effectively addressing these complications and improving the quality of life in children with cataracts.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Other Clinical Implications</bold>
</p>
        <p>The morbidity associated with pediatric cataracts extends beyond lens opacity and includes developmental, surgical, and lifelong sequelae. Untreated pediatric cataract leads to disease-related complications driven by visual deprivation during the critical period of visual development. Amblyopia is the most common consequence and is often irreversible without timely intervention, while early bilateral deprivation may result in nystagmus with poor visual prognosis. Sensory asymmetry frequently leads to strabismus, resulting in functional impairment and cosmetic concerns. Prolonged deprivation contributes to delayed visual maturation and reduced visual potential. Late presentation or lack of intervention may lead to irreversible blindness, representing a major public health burden.</p>
        <p>Preoperative and diagnostic complications include misdiagnosis of leukocoria, most critically due to failure to exclude retinoblastoma or PFV. Missed retinoblastoma carries life-threatening consequences, underscoring the need for urgent and thorough evaluation of any abnormal red reflex before proceeding with cataract management. During pediatric cataract surgery, posterior capsule rupture may occur because of a thin capsule or excessive surgical manipulation, increasing the risk of VAO and secondary glaucoma. Zonular dialysis, resulting from congenital zonular weakness or trauma, compromises capsular support and predisposes to IOL instability. Hyphema may develop from intraoperative iris trauma and can lead to secondary inflammation or glaucoma. Vitreous loss, most often associated with capsular rupture, is linked to higher rates of postoperative complications and more complex postoperative management.</p>
        <p>In the early postoperative period, excessive inflammation commonly occurs because of the robust inflammatory response characteristic of pediatric eyes, leading to synechiae formation and secondary membranes. VAO, driven by the proliferation of residual lens epithelial cells, represents the most frequent postoperative complication in children. Early-onset secondary glaucoma may develop as a result of angle damage or pupillary block and constitutes a sight-threatening condition with lifelong implications. Endophthalmitis, although rare, is a vision-threatening postoperative emergency related to surgical infection. Wound leaks from poor healing may result in hypotony and increase the risk of infection.</p>
        <p>Late postoperative complications include secondary glaucoma with delayed onset, attributed to angle recession and altered aqueous dynamics, which may manifest years after surgery. IOL decentration or dislocation may occur due to capsular contraction or zonular weakness, leading to visual disturbance and, in some cases, the need for reoperation. Refractive surprise or progressive myopic shift results from continued ocular growth in children and necessitates frequent refractive reassessment and optical adjustment. PCO develops from residual lens epithelial cell proliferation and causes reduced vision, while retinal detachment, although rare, arises from vitreoretinal traction and represents a vision-threatening complication.</p>
        <p>Optical and rehabilitation-related complications significantly influence long-term outcomes. Poor compliance with contact lens wear, often related to caregiver burden or cost, contributes to suboptimal visual rehabilitation. Spectacle intolerance, commonly due to anisometropia or magnification effects, impairs binocular vision. Recurrent amblyopia may develop in the setting of inconsistent patching and typically requires prolonged and closely supervised therapy.</p>
        <p>Medication-related complications include steroid-induced glaucoma, reflecting heightened reminder responsiveness in children and necessitating close IOP monitoring, as well as steroid-induced cataract progression associated with prolonged corticosteroid exposure and worsening lens opacity. Atropine toxicity may occur due to systemic absorption, presenting with fever, flushing, and central nervous system effects. Systemic and long-term consequences extend beyond ocular morbidity. The psychosocial impact of childhood visual disability contributes to educational difficulties and social challenges. A substantial economic burden results from lifelong treatment, frequent follow-up, and rehabilitative needs, affecting both family resources and broader societal support systems.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s18" sec-type="Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care">
        <title>Postoperative and Rehabilitation Care</title>
        <p>Early visual rehabilitation of children with pediatric cataracts is as crucial as early diagnosis and treatment.&#x000a0;Rehabilitation&#x000a0;reduces the incidence of amblyopia, strabismus, and poor fusion. Some options for visual rehabilitation in these children are spectacles,&#x000a0;contact lenses, IOLs, epikeratophakia, low vision aids, and amblyopia therapy.&#x000a0;Parents should be counseled on the importance of regular follow-up and routine refraction, as refractive changes are frequent during ocular growth.</p>
        <p>Children younger than 2 require aphakic glasses or contact lenses for visual rehabilitation after cataract surgery until secondary IOL implantation is considered. Aphakic glasses are typically&#x000a0;recommended for bilateral aphakia. However, the use of&#x000a0;these vision aids&#x000a0;has declined with advancements in surgical techniques and the increasing prevalence of primary IOL implantation. The goal of aphakia management is to provide appropriate optical correction following cataract extraction to enable symmetrical stimulation of the visual system during its critical period of development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r191">[191]</xref></p>
        <p>Spectacles are the earliest form of aphakia treatment. Aphakic glasses with bifocal addition remain the most commonly used option for visual rehabilitation in children with bilateral aphakia. These devices are&#x000a0;affordable and&#x000a0;accessible, and they do not&#x000a0;cause ocular discomfort or require direct contact with the eye's surface. However, these visual aids are limited by considerable weight and bulk, restricted peripheral vision, optical distortions&#x02014;including the jack-in-the-box phenomenon, pincushion effect, and ring scotoma&#x02014;and potential aggravation of nystagmus. In unilateral cases, spectacles are less suitable because anisometropia and aniseikonia can lead to diplopia and amblyopia, which may result in permanent suppression and anomalous retinal correspondence.</p>
        <p>Contact lenses are the most effective optical correction for postoperative aphakia. These lenses should be worn during waking hours and removed at night. Contact lenses are superior to spectacles in both unilateral and bilateral aphakia, offering a wider visual field, reduced glare and spherical aberrations, improved binocularity, and better cosmetic appearance. However, poor tolerance, cost, and nonadherence are common challenges, particularly among younger children. Chronic eye disease and ocular surface disorders are contraindications to the procedure.</p>
        <p>Available options include polymethylmethacrylate, rigid gas-permeable, silicone elastomer, and hydrogel lenses. Extended-wear lenses are often preferred, though their use is limited by complications such as giant papillary conjunctivitis, neovascularization, corneal abrasions, and infectious keratitis, making daily-wear lenses the safer alternative. Children older than 2 with primary IOL implantation still require adequate visual rehabilitation after surgery. Spectacles or contact lenses are prescribed after appropriate refraction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r192">[192]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Amblyopia Treatment</bold>
</p>
        <p>Deprivational amblyopia is highly prevalent in pediatric cataracts, particularly during the critical period of visual development in the first 2 months of life, due to unequal visual input between the eyes. Unilateral cataracts generally produce more severe amblyopia than bilateral cases, necessitating prompt surgical intervention. Early initiation of amblyopia therapy is essential for visual rehabilitation, with occlusion therapy&#x02014;patching the better eye per ATS protocols&#x02014;serving as the primary modality. Pharmacologic penalization with atropine provides an alternative in selected cases. Ensuring adherence to therapy is often challenging, making caregiver counseling and consistent follow-up critical for effective treatment. Patients require reevaluation every 2 weeks to assess visual improvement and monitor for reversal of amblyopia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r193">[193]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Low Vision Aids</bold>
</p>
        <p>These devices help children maximize their visual performance and support their developmental potential. A wide range of tools is available, depending on the child&#x02019;s age and the family's financial capacity. Convex lenses are the primary optical aids used in various forms. Other commonly used devices include bifocals, hand magnifiers, stand magnifiers, electronic magnifiers, telescopes, and computer-based closed-circuit televisions.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Postoperative Care</bold>
</p>
        <p>Effective postoperative management ensures early detection of complications and facilitates proper visual rehabilitation. The specific interventions and monitoring strategies for each stage are detailed below.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Immediate phase</bold>
</p>
        <p>Immediate postoperative management (days 0&#x02013;7) requires vigilant monitoring for early complications, including infection,&#x000a0;IOP elevation, inflammation, and wound integrity issues. Intensive topical corticosteroids, initially administered hourly and then tapered, control the robust pediatric inflammatory response. Cycloplegic agents, such as atropine or cyclopentolate, reduce pain and prevent posterior synechiae formation. Broad-spectrum topical antibiotics are essential to prevent postoperative infection.</p>
        <p>Early and regular IOP assessment allows prompt detection of secondary glaucoma. Wound integrity should be assessed by slit-lamp examination to identify complications, such as wound leakage or iris prolapse. Consistent administration of prescribed medications and proactive monitoring are critical to ensuring optimal recovery and maintaining postoperative comfort.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r194">[194]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Early&#x000a0;phase</bold>
</p>
        <p>During the early postoperative period (weeks&#x000a0;1&#x02013;6), corticosteroids are tapered gradually based on the degree of inflammation to prevent rebound uveitis. Slit-lamp and red reflex examinations allow early detection of VAO. Initial refraction is performed once the cornea stabilizes to enable timely optical correction. Serial IOP measurements monitor for steroid-induced or aphakic glaucoma.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Intermediate phase</bold>
</p>
        <p>Visual development during this stage (weeks 6 to 1 year) is monitored using age-appropriate visual acuity testing to assess maturation. Orthoptic evaluation enables early detection and management of strabismus. Slit-lamp examination assesses IOL position to identify decentration or tilt. Periodic refraction addresses myopic shift and ensures optimal optical correction.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Long-term care</bold>
</p>
        <p>Lifelong&#x000a0;glaucoma surveillance (1&#x000a0;year after surgery onward) involves regular IOP measurement and optic nerve evaluation, as glaucoma may develop years after surgery. Secondary procedures, including YAG capsulotomy or membranectomy, restore visual axis clarity when needed. Strabismus surgery corrects ocular misalignment, improving binocularity and cosmesis. Low-vision rehabilitation with visual aids and training enhances functional vision.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Follow-Up Visits</bold>
</p>
        <p>Regular follow-up with a pediatric ophthalmologist is necessary to monitor wound healing and identify complications at an early stage. Visual acuity must be assessed during each visit to evaluate surgical outcomes and determine the need for further intervention. IOP should also be measured routinely to detect the onset of postoperative glaucoma, a known complication.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r195">[195]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Secondary Interventions</bold>
</p>
        <p>PCO is a frequent delayed complication and may require treatment with Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy. Strabismus, if present or persistent after surgery, should be managed with appropriate therapeutic strategies to restore ocular alignment.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Rehabilitation Care</bold>
</p>
        <p>Postoperative rehabilitation is integral to achieving favorable visual outcomes. Effective rehabilitation includes targeted visual interventions and developmental support strategies.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Visual rehabilitation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Corrective lenses are essential for addressing residual refractive errors and optimizing visual development. Infants with aphakia require contact lenses, while aphakic spectacles may be used when appropriate to restore a focused retinal image. Children with pseudophakia benefit from age-appropriate spectacles to correct residual refractive error and optimize visual acuity. High-index lenses or contact lenses are recommended for anisometropia to improve binocular vision. Amblyopia treatment remains crucial, with occlusion therapy, such as patching the dominant eye, enhancing the weaker eye&#x02019;s function, and supporting binocular development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r196">[196]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Occupational therapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Visual skills training helps the child adjust to altered visual input and strengthens functional vision in daily tasks. Developmental support should be provided to children with visual impairments and associated delays to ensure continued progress toward age-appropriate milestones.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r197">[197]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Family&#x000a0;Education and Support</bold>
</p>
        <p>Educating parents about postoperative care is critical to successful outcomes. Parental counseling provides detailed instruction on medication administration, patch application, and follow-up schedules, thereby improving adherence and ensuring continuity of treatment. Clear guidance on recognizing early signs of complications supports timely intervention. Long-term management requires consistent adherence to prescribed therapies to optimize visual development. Psychosocial support addresses emotional and social challenges, enhancing quality of life, and access to counseling services or support groups helps families navigate the complexities of pediatric cataract care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r198">[198]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>School and Social Integration</bold>
</p>
        <p>Educational support ensures that children with visual impairments receive necessary accommodations in the classroom to enhance academic performance and promote inclusion. Structured opportunities for social interaction should be encouraged to promote peer engagement and facilitate the development of social skills, thereby improving quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r199">[199]</xref>&#x000a0; Effective postoperative and rehabilitative care for pediatric cataracts requires a comprehensive approach that incorporates regular monitoring, timely intervention, visual rehabilitation, and sustained support for both the child and family. Addressing both medical and developmental needs enables healthcare providers to optimize visual outcomes and enhance overall quality of life for patients with this condition.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s19" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>A pediatric cataract may occur in isolation or as part of a systemic disease involving other organ systems, such as the cardiac, renal, or central nervous systems. Effective management of pediatric cataracts often necessitates an interprofessional approach, involving consultations with various healthcare professionals to ensure comprehensive care. A pediatric ophthalmologist (or cornea specialist) plays a central role in diagnosing, performing surgery, and managing postoperative care for pediatric cataracts. The expertise of these providers in pediatric-specific surgical techniques is critical for achieving successful visual rehabilitation. Consultation with a pediatric ophthalmologist should be initiated promptly upon suspicion or diagnosis to facilitate timely intervention and prevent long-term visual impairment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r200">[200]</xref></p>
        <p>The pediatrician (eg, neonatologist) is essential for overall health evaluation, including the exclusion of systemic comorbidities and gross congenital anomalies that may contribute to cataract development. Pediatricians coordinate with specialists to manage these conditions and ensure integrated care. Routine pediatric examinations should include screening for ocular abnormalities, with timely referral to a pediatric ophthalmologist if cataract signs are detected. If cataracts are suspected to be hereditary, a genetic counselor can provide necessary guidance on recurrence risk in future pregnancies and facilitate genetic testing. Consultation should be considered when a family history of cataracts or a suspected genetic syndrome is present.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r201">[201]</xref></p>
        <p>Given the need for surgical intervention in many cases of pediatric cataracts, an anesthesiologist experienced in pediatric care is essential to ensure safe anesthesia management. Since general anesthesia is required, a thorough preanesthetic evaluation must be conducted to assess the child&#x02019;s overall health and minimize the risk of intraoperative complications. An anesthesia consultation should occur well before surgery to plan safe anesthetic administration during the procedure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r202">[202]</xref></p>
        <p>Consultation with a metabolic specialist or endocrinologist is indicated when a metabolic cataract is suspected, particularly to evaluate for galactosemia, diabetes mellitus, or hypocalcemia, as early diagnosis and management help prevent systemic complications. An infectious disease specialist is warranted when TORCH or other congenital infections are suspected, with responsibilities including serologic testing and infection management to limit disease progression and systemic morbidity. Optometrists (or refraction specialists) assist in the postoperative care of children with cataracts by prescribing corrective lenses and monitoring visual development. These professionals play a vital role in ongoing visual rehabilitation. Regular postoperative consultations should be scheduled to ensure proper correction and to detect any changes in vision.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r203">[203]</xref></p>
        <p>Occupational therapists support visual rehabilitation by helping children adapt to visual changes and improve their functional abilities in daily activities. Referral may be particularly beneficial for children with significant visual impairment following surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r204">[204]</xref>&#x000a0;An orthoptist plays an important role in cases complicated by strabismus or amblyopia, providing amblyopia therapy and binocular vision assessment to improve functional visual outcomes. A glaucoma specialist should be involved when IOP is elevated or long-term glaucoma risk is anticipated, as ongoing surveillance and treatment are essential to prevent optic nerve damage. Referral to a low-vision specialist is appropriate for children with poor visual outcomes or ocular anomalies, with a focus on visual aids and rehabilitation strategies to enhance quality of life.</p>
        <p>Emotional and psychological support for the child and family is essential, primarily when cataracts significantly affect the child&#x02019;s quality of life. Social workers or psychologists can provide counseling and connect families with support resources. Consultation should be considered early in the course of treatment to ensure comprehensive support. A pediatric neurologist should be consulted when developmental delay or central nervous system anomalies are present, to facilitate formal neurological assessment and identify associated neurodevelopmental disorders. Social workers or counselors are essential when socioeconomic barriers affect care, as they provide family support and facilitate adherence to treatment and follow-up. Engagement with public health services or nongovernmental organizations is particularly valuable for children from rural or underserved populations, as it supports awareness initiatives and provides financial assistance to reduce preventable childhood blindness.</p>
        <p>Managing pediatric cataracts requires a comprehensive, interprofessional approach that extends beyond ophthalmologic care. Timely consultation with pediatricians, genetic counselors, anesthesiologists, optometrists, occupational therapists, and mental health professionals ensures holistic, individualized care. This collaborative model addresses all aspects of the child&#x02019;s health and development, resulting in improved visual and overall outcomes for both children and their families.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s20" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Parents must be advised&#x000a0;regarding the nature and etiology of their child&#x02019;s condition, available treatment options, and visual prognosis. Counseling should also include a discussion of potential surgical complications and the importance of regular follow-up and appropriate visual rehabilitation to optimize visual outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Deterrence</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Protecting maternal and infant health during pregnancy and early childhood is critical for preventing pediatric cataracts. Mitigative&#x000a0;strategies should address modifiable risk factors that contribute to cataract development in newborns and young children. Encouraging maternal vaccination against infections known to cause congenital cataracts, such as rubella, and minimizing exposure to pathogens like <italic toggle="yes">T gondii</italic> and&#x000a0;CMV can reduce the risk of intrauterine infection. Adequate maternal nutrition, particularly intake of vitamin A, folic acid, and &#x003c9;-3 fatty acids, supports normal fetal eye development. Regular prenatal checkups allow early identification and management of maternal or fetal issues that may affect ocular development.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r205">[205]</xref></p>
        <p>Routine newborn eye screenings, including the red reflex test, facilitate early identification of congenital cataracts and improve visual outcomes through timely intervention. Genetic counseling is recommended for families with a history of pediatric cataracts or known genetic disorders to guide risk assessment and early detection strategies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r206">[206]</xref>&#x000a0;Metabolic disorders such as galactosemia, diabetes, and hypocalcemia should be included in newborn screening protocols, as these conditions may lead to cataract formation if left untreated. Children with identified systemic or metabolic diseases require ongoing ophthalmic monitoring to detect and manage cataracts at the earliest stage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r207">[207]</xref>&#x000a0;Reducing the incidence of pediatric cataracts requires an integrated approach targeting prenatal care, infection prevention, maternal nutrition, genetic assessment, and early detection and management of systemic conditions.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Patient&#x000a0;Education</bold>
</p>
        <p>Educating parents and caregivers about pediatric cataracts is essential to support early detection, timely intervention, and effective postoperative care.&#x000a0;Education should cover the nature of the condition, including its causes, symptoms, and the need for early detection, using clear, nontechnical language to ensure comprehension. Emphasizing the importance of timely treatment helps prevent long-term visual impairment and improves outcomes for both surgical and medical interventions.</p>
        <p>Postoperative care must be reinforced through education on the necessity of attending all scheduled follow-up appointments to monitor recovery and detect complications. Parents should be made fully aware of the importance of adhering to prescribed medication regimens, including antibiotics and anti-inflammatory agents, to prevent postoperative infections and inflammation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r208">[208]</xref>&#x000a0;Visual rehabilitation also requires thorough guidance. Parents must understand the role of corrective lenses, whether glasses or contact lenses, and the importance of occlusion therapy (patching) in improving visual outcomes and preventing amblyopia. Information on available visual rehabilitation programs and community resources should be provided to enhance the child&#x02019;s visual development.</p>
        <p>Support should extend beyond medical needs. Families benefit from access to emotional and psychological support services to help manage the stress of caring for a visually impaired child.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r209">[209]</xref> Educational materials, such as brochures, videos, and digital resources, can reinforce learning and provide a foundation for ongoing parental engagement in the child&#x02019;s care. By focusing on prevention through prenatal care and early detection and providing thorough patient education, healthcare providers can&#x000a0;significantly improve the management and outcomes of pediatric cataracts. This comprehensive approach ensures that children receive the best possible care, leading to better visual health and quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r210">[210]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s21" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>
<bold>Pearls in Managing Pediatric Cataracts</bold>
</p>
        <p>Early detection and intervention are critical in managing pediatric cataracts, with diagnosis ideally made within the first few weeks of life. Routine red reflex testing should be integrated into newborn screening protocols to ensure timely identification. Treatment plans must be individualized based on cataract type, severity, age at presentation, and any associated conditions. This personalized strategy guides surgical planning and IOL selection to best support visual development. Interprofessional collaboration among pediatric ophthalmologists, pediatricians, anesthesiologists, optometrists, and support staff is essential. Coordinated care from this team addresses the full spectrum of management, supporting both visual outcomes and overall well-being.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Disposition</bold>
</p>
        <p>Comprehensive preoperative evaluation is essential and should include a detailed ocular examination, assessment of visual acuity, and evaluation for any underlying systemic conditions. The timing of surgery is critical. Surgical intervention is recommended between 6 and 8 weeks of age for bilateral significant cataracts and&#x000a0;between 4 and 6 weeks for unilateral cataracts. Appropriate anesthesia and diligent postoperative care are vital to minimizing complications. Regular follow-up is necessary to monitor for conditions such as glaucoma, PCO, and amblyopia. Visual rehabilitation, using glasses, contact lenses, or patching, is crucial to achieve optimal visual outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r211">[211]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Pitfalls</bold>
</p>
        <p>Delayed diagnosis of pediatric cataracts can result in irreversible visual impairment. Routine check-ups must include vigilant screening to facilitate early detection. Inadequate postoperative management, particularly failure to address complications such as IOP elevation, uveitis, or secondary cataract formation, may compromise visual outcomes. Close postoperative monitoring is essential. Noncompliance with follow-up visits and prescribed treatments, including corrective lenses and occlusion therapy, significantly increases the risk of poor visual development and amblyopia. Parental education is necessary to ensure adherence.</p>
        <p>Genetic counseling helps families understand the implications of hereditary cataracts, and prenatal screening may be appropriate for at-risk families.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r212">[212]</xref>&#x000a0;Preventing maternal infections during pregnancy, particularly those caused by the TORCHES group, through vaccination and adequate prenatal care can reduce the risk of congenital cataracts. Early identification and treatment of metabolic disorders such as galactosemia and diabetes are also critical, with newborn screening programs playing a key role in early diagnosis and prevention.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r213">[213]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Additional Information</bold>
</p>
        <p>Technological advancements in surgical techniques and IOL materials have significantly improved the outcomes of pediatric cataract procedures. Innovations such as femtosecond laser-assisted cataract surgery and newer IOLs have enhanced surgical precision and visual outcomes. Family education plays a vital role in ensuring successful treatment. Clear information about the condition, available therapies, and the necessity of compliance with postoperative care must be provided.</p>
        <p>Psychological support is also beneficial in helping families navigate the emotional and practical challenges of diagnosis and management. Ongoing research into the genetic and molecular basis of pediatric cataracts is crucial for developing new therapeutic and preventive approaches. Participation in clinical trials and registries contributes to advancing understanding and care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r214">[214]</xref> Emphasizing these areas enables healthcare teams to deliver more effective management and improve long-term visual outcomes and quality of life for affected children.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-134015.s22" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Pediatric cataracts are often first identified in primary care settings, typically when concerned parents observe leukocoria, ocular misalignment, or nystagmus. Primary healthcare clinicians play a critical role in promptly referring these children to specialized centers equipped to manage pediatric cataracts. A thorough systemic and ocular evaluation is essential to exclude other causes of leukocoria and identify any syndromic associations before surgery. Early diagnosis, timely management, and appropriate visual rehabilitation are key determinants of a favorable prognosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r215">[215]</xref>&#x000a0;An interprofessional team approach&#x000a0;involving pediatric ophthalmologists, pediatricians, anesthesiologists, optometrists, nurses, and engaged, well-informed parents is essential for achieving optimal visual outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Skills and Strategy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Effective management of pediatric cataracts demands specialized expertise and a coordinated strategy. Pediatric ophthalmologists must be adept at diagnosing and surgically treating congenital and developmental cataracts. Optometrists and pediatricians are instrumental in early detection and timely referral. Nurses require training in perioperative care to support children throughout the treatment process. Pharmacists contribute by ensuring appropriate medication use, particularly when antifungal or antibiotic therapy is needed. A well-executed plan emphasizing early intervention and continuous follow-up is critical to preventing long-term visual disability.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ethics and Responsibilities</bold>
</p>
        <p>Ethical practice is essential in the management of pediatric cataracts. Healthcare professionals must obtain informed consent from parents or guardians by clearly explaining the risks, benefits, and treatment alternatives. The family's preferences and cultural beliefs should be respected while ensuring decisions prioritize the child&#x02019;s best interest. Responsibilities also include maintaining confidentiality, delivering equitable care, and addressing ethical challenges, such as surgical timing and managing cases with limited prognostic value.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r216">[216]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Interprofessional Communication</bold>
</p>
        <p>Clear and effective communication among healthcare team members is critical. Regular team meetings and standardized tools such as Situation-Background-Assessment-Recommendation help align care plans. Digital platforms, including electronic health records, support real-time information sharing and coordination among physicians, nurses, pharmacists, and other involved specialists.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r217">[217]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Care Coordination</bold>
</p>
        <p>Care coordination is essential for delivering comprehensive, continuous care to children with pediatric cataracts. This process requires collaboration among healthcare providers to develop and implement individualized care plans. Case managers or care coordinators play a key role in navigating the healthcare system, facilitating appointments, and ensuring timely follow-up. A coordinated approach addresses not only the ocular condition but also any comorbidities, supporting adherence to treatment regimens and improving overall care delivery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r218">[218]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Patient-Centered Care</bold>
</p>
        <p>Patient-centered care emphasizes the specific needs of each child and family. This approach involves engaging families in shared decision-making, educating them about pediatric cataracts and treatment options, and providing access to support services. Tailoring care to the child's developmental stage while addressing emotional and social concerns strengthens the therapeutic alliance and promotes treatment adherence. Patient-centered care enhances both satisfaction and clinical outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r219">[219]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Outcomes and Patient Safety</bold>
</p>
        <p>Improving outcomes and maintaining patient safety are central to pediatric cataract management. Priorities include minimizing surgical complications, preventing postoperative infections, and ensuring safe and accurate medication administration. Applying evidence-based techniques, such as using sharp-edged IOLs to reduce VAO, improves visual outcomes. Continuous training and strict adherence to safety protocols are critical to upholding quality standards in care delivery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r220">[220]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Team Performance</bold>
</p>
        <p>Enhancing team performance requires ongoing training, regular performance assessments, and fostering a collaborative work environment. Open communication and recognition of each team member&#x02019;s contributions are key to fostering effective teamwork. The use of performance metrics allows teams to monitor outcomes and identify areas needing improvement, supporting quality improvement efforts. Opportunities for professional development ensure that all members remain current with advancements in pediatric cataract management. Effective management of pediatric cataracts demands an interprofessional approach that integrates specialized clinical skills, strategic planning, ethical decision-making, clear communication, coordinated care delivery, patient-centered practices, and continuous performance improvement. Prioritizing these elements enables healthcare teams to deliver safe, high-quality care, resulting in improved visual and developmental outcomes for children with pediatric cataracts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-134015.r221">[221]</xref></p>
      </sec>
