      <floats-group id="article-134015.s25">
        <fig id="article-134015.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Pediatric Cataract, Zonular Type. This slit-lamp photograph depicts the zonular type of pediatric cataract with a blue dot cataract component. Contributed by Dr. Priyadarshi Gupta, MBBS, (MS) Ophthalmology</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Zonular__cataract__with__blue__dot__cataract__component" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-134015.s26">
        <fig id="article-134015.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Pediatric Cataract, Posterior Lenticonus.&#x000a0;Slit-lamp photograph showing a posterior lenticonus variant of pediatric cataract. Located near the eye's nodal point, posterior lenticonus typically impairs visual acuity more than anterior opacities. This form is frequently associated with Lowe syndrome. Contributed by Dr. Priyadarshi Gupta, MBBS, (MS) Ophthalmology</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Posterior__lenticonus" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-134015.s27">
        <fig id="article-134015.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Congenital Cataracts and Abnormal Galactose Metabolism.&#x000a0;Peripheral lenticular opacities of this type are often seen in patients with galactosemia or galactokinase deficiency. Contributed by Prof. Bhupendra C. K. Patel MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Screen__Shot__2021-06-02__at__12.30.47__PM" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-134015.s28">
        <fig id="article-134015.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Red Reflex.&#x000a0;This image shows a&#x000a0;normal red reflex in each eye with equal clarity and brightness. The red reflex indicates the absence of opacification of the globe structures: cornea, aqueous, lens, vitreous, and a normal retina. Contributed by Prof. Bhupendra C. K. Patel MD, FRCS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Screen__Shot__2021-06-06__at__8.45.27__PM" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
